@node Smalltalk Forms and Constructs
@section Smalltalk Forms and Constructs

Smalltalk is a dynamically-typed, purely object-oriented language where everything is an object. This section documents the core forms used in Smalltalk programming.

@node Smalltalk Message Sends
@subsection Message Sends

@strong{Definition:} A message send is the fundamental mechanism of communication between objects in Smalltalk. It follows the pattern @code{receiver message:} arguments where the receiver is an object, the message selector identifies the method, and arguments are passed to that method. Message sends are more than function calls---they represent an ontological commitment to object-oriented computation where all behavior emerges from objects communicating via messages.

@strong{Syntax:}
@example
@var{receiver} @var{messageSelector}
@var{receiver} @var{messageSelector:} @var{argument}
@var{receiver} @var{messageSelector:} @var{arg1} @var{andSelector:} @var{arg2}
@var{receiver} @var{selector1:} @var{arg1} @var{selector2:} @var{arg2}
@var{receiver} @var{selector1:} @var{arg1} @var{selector2:} @var{arg2} @var{selector3:} @var{arg3}
@end example

@strong{Numeric Type Support:}
Message sends work with all numeric types and their associated operations:
@itemize
@item BINARY: Integer message sends on SmallInteger objects, bit operations (@code{bitAnd:}, @code{bitOr:}, @code{bitXor:}, @code{bitShift:}), and binary arithmetic messages
@item DECIMAL: Floating-point arithmetic messages on Float objects, transcendental functions (@code{sqrt}, @code{sin}, @code{cos}), and precision handling
@item DISPLAY: Display and formatting messages that convert numeric values to user-visible representations through @code{asString}, @code{printOn:}, and @code{displayString}
@end itemize

@strong{Examples:}

@example
" Unary message - returns string length "
'hello' size.

" Binary message with argument - uses infix notation "
10 + 5.
0b1111 - 0b0001.
3.14 * 2.0.

" Keyword message with single argument "
player moveTo: position.
sprite setColor: #red.

" Keyword message with multiple arguments "
game playSound: #explosion 
       atVolume: 0.8
       withPitch: 1.2.
      inChannel: 1.

" Message send with binary numeric literals "
0b1010 + 0b0011.
0b11110000 bitAnd: 0b10101010.

" Decimal arithmetic messages "
3.14 * radius.
circumference := 2.0 * 3.14159 * radius.

" Chained message sends (cascade) "
(player position) distance: (enemy position).
player setX: 100 y: 200.
collection add: item1; add: item2; add: item3.

" Message to expression result "
(10 + 20) * 3.
((array at: 1) + (array at: 2)) asString.

" Message to self "
self doSomething.
self processData: input.

" Message to super (invoke superclass method) "
super initialize.
@end example

@strong{Paradigm Explanation:}

Message sends embody Smalltalk's object-oriented principle that computation happens through communication between objects. Rather than function calls with procedures, Smalltalk employs late binding---the method to execute is determined at runtime based on the receiver's class. This enables polymorphism, where different classes can implement the same message differently. Unary messages require no parentheses; binary messages use infix notation (+, -, *, /); keyword messages use colons after each keyword component. The cascade operator (;) allows sending multiple messages to the same receiver sequentially. This design creates a uniform model of computation centered on object interaction rather than imperative procedure.

Message sends in Smalltalk enable sophisticated programming patterns. The receiver expression is always evaluated first, then the message selector is matched to a method in the receiver's class. If the method is not found, the search continues up the inheritance hierarchy. This late binding enables dynamic dispatch and polymorphic behavior. Message sends can be nested arbitrarily and combined with control structures, creating expressive and flexible code.

@node Smalltalk Conditionals
@subsection Conditionals (ifTrue:, ifFalse:, ifTrue:ifFalse:)

@strong{Definition:} Conditionals evaluate a Boolean expression and execute different code blocks based on the result. The receiver is a Boolean value (true/false), and the message determines which block executes.

@strong{Syntax:}
@example
@var{condition} ifTrue: [@var{trueBlock}].
@var{condition} ifFalse: [@var{falseBlock}].
@var{condition} ifTrue: [@var{trueBlock}] ifFalse: [@var{falseBlock}].
(@var{condition}) ifTrue: [@var{trueBlock}] ifFalse: [@var{falseBlock}].
@end example

@strong{Numeric Type Support:}
@itemize
@item BINARY: Bitwise conditions (= 0, ~= 0 checks)
@item DECIMAL: Floating-point comparisons
@item DISPLAY: Display-based conditions
@end itemize

@strong{Examples:}

@example
" Simple if-true with numeric comparison "
(health > 0) ifTrue: [
  player moveForward.
].

" If-false example "
(ammo = 0) ifFalse: [
  weapon fire.
].

" Combined if-true-if-false "
(score > highScore) 
  ifTrue: [self displayNewRecord]
  ifFalse: [self displayCurrentScore].

" Nested conditionals "
(level > 5) ifTrue: [
  (difficulty = #hard) ifTrue: [
    game spawnBoss.
  ] ifFalse: [
    game spawnEnemy.
  ].
].

" Binary numeric conditional "
(0b1111 = mask) ifTrue: [
  self activateAll.
].

" Decimal comparison "
(temperature > 98.6) ifTrue: [
  alarm ring.
].
@end example

@strong{Paradigm Explanation:}

In Smalltalk, conditionals are not control flow statements but message sends to Boolean objects. The receiver is the condition result, and the message (ifTrue:, ifFalse:, or ifTrue:ifFalse:) determines execution. The blocks (enclosed in square brackets) are lazy-evaluated---passed as BlockClosure objects and only executed if the message requires it. This approach provides greater flexibility than traditional if-statements and allows custom control structures. The programmer can define new control structures by creating methods that take blocks as arguments, enabling domain-specific extensions to the language. This is impossible in languages where conditionals are syntactic keywords rather than message sends.

The lazy evaluation of blocks is critical to conditional implementation. If blocks were eagerly evaluated, conditionals would not work correctly---both branches would execute regardless of the condition. By deferring evaluation, Smalltalk conditionals follow the same lazy-evaluation pattern as traditional conditional evaluation. This also enables short-circuit evaluation: in @code{condition1 and: condition2}, if condition1 is false, the block containing condition2 is never evaluated, avoiding unnecessary computation and potential side effects.

Nested conditionals can be composed elegantly. Instead of writing @code{if x then if y then ... end if end if}, Smalltalk's message-based conditionals allow the true-branch to be a message send that itself contains another conditional. This composability is a direct consequence of treating control flow as object communication.

@node Smalltalk Loops
@subsection Loops (whileTrue:, whileFalse:, to:do:, timesRepeat:)

@strong{Definition:} Loops repeat a code block multiple times under various conditions. Smalltalk provides several loop constructs: conditional loops (whileTrue:/whileFalse:), numeric iteration (to:do:), and repetition (timesRepeat:). Like conditionals, loops are implemented as message sends to block objects, enabling lazy evaluation and custom loop semantics through method definition.

@strong{Syntax:}
@example
[@var{condition}] whileTrue: [@var{loopBlock}].
[@var{condition}] whileFalse: [@var{loopBlock}].
@var{start} to: @var{end} do: [:@var{index} | @var{loopBody}].
@var{count} timesRepeat: [@var{loopBlock}].
@var{collection} do: [:element | @var{loopBody}].
@end example

@strong{Numeric Type Support:}
@itemize
@item BINARY: Loop indices as binary integers, bitwise operations in loop body
@item DECIMAL: Numeric ranges with decimals (coerced to integers for indexing), decimal calculations within loops
@item DISPLAY: Loop display operations, accumulation of display strings
@end itemize

@strong{Examples:}

@example
" Repeat block while condition is true "
x := 0.
[x < 100] whileTrue: [
  x := x + 1.
  y := y * 2.
].

" Repeat while condition is false (negated condition) "
[morph isVisible] whileFalse: [
  morph show.
].

" Numeric range iteration with index "
1 to: 10 do: [:i |
  Transcript show: i; cr.
].

" Binary range iteration "
0b0001 to: 0b1111 do: [:i |
  self processBit: i.
].

" Fixed repetition count - simple repeat "
5 timesRepeat: [
  sound play.
].

" Nested loops with multiple indices "
1 to: 10 do: [:i |
  1 to: 10 do: [:j |
    grid at: i at: j put: (i * j).
  ].
].

" Loop with early termination (collector pattern) "
| result |
result := OrderedCollection new.
[true] whileTrue: [
  | value |
  value := self getNextValue.
  (value = #stop) ifTrue: [^result].
  result add: value.
].

" Loop with decimal to integer coercion "
1 to: 10.0 do: [:i |
  self applyEffect: i.
].

" Collection iteration with do: "
#(apple banana cherry) do: [:fruit |
  Transcript show: fruit; cr.
].

" Complex loop with state accumulation "
| sum count average |
sum := 0.
count := 0.
1 to: 100 do: [:i |
  sum := sum + i.
  count := count + 1.
].
average := sum / count.
@end example

@strong{Paradigm Explanation:}

Loops in Smalltalk preserve object-oriented design through message sends to BlockClosure objects, maintaining consistency with Smalltalk's everything-is-an-object philosophy. A @code{whileTrue:} loop repeatedly executes a condition block; if true, it executes the loop block and repeats immediately. The @code{to:do:} construct provides convenient numeric iteration without explicit loop variable management, with the index automatically passed to the block parameter. The @code{timesRepeat:} form simplifies fixed repetitions. Unlike imperative loops that modify mutable loop variables, Smalltalk loops are expressed as messages to immutable integers or blocks, shifting the focus from state management to behavior specification. This design enables loop composition: loops can be passed as arguments, returned from methods, and combined with other blocks. The lazy evaluation of blocks is fundamental: the condition block is re-evaluated before each iteration, and the loop body is only executed if the condition remains true. This lazy evaluation enables complex termination conditions and dynamic loop adaptation.

@node Smalltalk Method Definition
@subsection Method Definition

@strong{Definition:} Methods define behavior for objects in a class. They specify the algorithm executed when a corresponding message is sent to an instance of that class. Methods are the locus of behavior in object-oriented Smalltalk; classes organize methods by the objects they operate on. Method definitions include method names, parameters, local variable declarations, and implementation bodies.

@strong{Syntax:}
@example
@var{methodName}
  " method body "
  ^result.

@var{keyword:} argument1 @var{and:} argument2
  " method body "
  ^result.

@var{methodName}: arg
  | localVar1 localVar2 |
  localVar1 := arg.
  ^localVar1 + 1.
@end example

@strong{Numeric Type Support:}
@itemize
@item BINARY: Methods returning binary integers, performing bitwise operations
@item DECIMAL: Methods with floating-point arithmetic and transcendental functions
@item DISPLAY: Methods for display formatting and string generation
@end itemize

@strong{Examples:}

@example
" Simple unary method returning a value "
getHealth
  ^health.

" Method with keyword parameter modifying state "
takeDamage: amount
  health := health - amount.
  (health <= 0) ifTrue: [self die].

" Method with multiple keyword parameters "
moveTo: newX and: newY
  x := newX.
  y := newY.

" Method with local variables and binary arithmetic "
calculateMask
  | result mask |
  result := 0b0000.
  mask := 0b1111.
  ^result + mask.

" Method with decimal calculations "
calculateDistance: otherX and: otherY
  | dx dy |
  dx := (x - otherX).
  dy := (y - otherY).
  ^((dx * dx) + (dy * dy)) sqrt.

" Method invoking other methods (message sends) "
heal: amount
  health := health + amount.
  (health > maxHealth) ifTrue: [
    health := maxHealth.
  ].
  self displayHealthBar.

" Method with conditional logic "
checkHealth
  (health > 50) ifTrue: [
    ^'healthy'
  ].
  (health > 0) ifTrue: [
    ^'wounded'
  ].
  ^'dead'.

" Method with collection iteration "
activateAll
  sprites do: [:sprite |
    sprite activate.
  ].

" Method with complex loop "
computeStats
  | sum count average |
  sum := 0.
  count := 0.
  values do: [:val |
    sum := sum + val.
    count := count + 1.
  ].
  average := sum / count.
  ^average.

" Private method (by convention starts with underscore) "
_internalCalculation: value
  | x y |
  x := value * 2.
  y := x + 10.
  ^y.

" Method returning self for method chaining "
enableFeature
  isEnabled := true.
  ^self.

" Method with binary operations on parameters "
applyMask: data with: mask
  | result |
  result := data bitAnd: mask.
  ^result.
@end example

@strong{Paradigm Explanation:}

Method definitions organize behavior into classes. Each method has a signature (name and parameter list) and a body (implementation). Unary methods require no parameters; keyword methods use colons after keywords; binary methods use infix operators. Local variables are declared between vertical bars (|var1 var2|); the caret (^) denotes explicit return. Smalltalk methods are dispatch units: when a message is sent to an object, the runtime looks up the method in the object's class and executes it. This lookup mechanism enables polymorphism: different classes can implement the same message with different behavior. Methods can call other methods via message sends (self methodName or object methodName), enabling method composition and abstraction. Methods returning self enable fluent interfaces where multiple method calls can be chained together. Private methods (by convention prefixed with underscore) are intended for internal class use, though Smalltalk lacks true access control. Methods can be overridden in subclasses, enabling inheritance and specialization of behavior.

@node Smalltalk Block Definition
@subsection Block Definition (BlockClosure)

@strong{Definition:} Blocks are deferred computations---code objects that can be stored, passed as arguments, and executed later. They capture their enclosing scope and support closure semantics.

@strong{Syntax:}
@example
[@var{statement1}. @var{statement2}].
[:@var{arg1} @var{arg2} | @var{statement}].
[| @var{localVar} | @var{statement}].
@end example

@strong{Numeric Type Support:}
@itemize
@item BINARY: Blocks returning binary results
@item DECIMAL: Blocks with floating-point operations
@item DISPLAY: Blocks for display operations
@end itemize

@strong{Examples:}

@example
" Simple block without parameters "
block := [
  Transcript show: 'Hello'.
  Transcript cr.
].
block value.

" Block with parameters "
addBlock := [:a :b | a + b].
result := addBlock value: 10 value: 20.

" Block capturing enclosing variables (closure) "
counter := 0.
incrementer := [counter := counter + 1. counter].
incrementer value.
incrementer value.

" Block with binary literal operations "
maskBlock := [:data |
  data bitAnd: 0b1111.
].
masked := maskBlock value: 0b11010011.

" Block with decimal operations "
averageBlock := [:a :b |
  (a + b) / 2.0.
].
avg := averageBlock value: 10.5 value: 20.7.

" Block with local variables "
sumBlock := [| sum |
  sum := 0.
  1 to: 10 do: [:i | sum := sum + i].
  sum.
].
total := sumBlock value.
@end example

@strong{Paradigm Explanation:}

Blocks are fundamental to Smalltalk's functional programming capabilities. They enable higher-order functions, allowing code to be parameterized by behavior. Blocks are first-class objects---they can be stored in variables, passed to methods, and executed via the `value` message. The pipe syntax (|...|) delineates parameters and local variables. Blocks naturally capture variables from their enclosing scope, enabling closures that maintain state across multiple invocations.

@node Smalltalk Assignment
@subsection Assignment Statements

@strong{Definition:} Assignment binds a variable to a value. The `:=` operator (read as ``becomes'') rebinds a variable to the right-hand side expression's result.

@strong{Syntax:}
@example
@var{variable} := @var{expression}.
@var{variable} := @var{variable} := @var{expression}.
@var{var1} := @var{var2} := @var{value}.
@end example

@strong{Numeric Type Support:}
@itemize
@item BINARY: Assignment of binary integer values
@item DECIMAL: Assignment of floating-point values
@item DISPLAY: Assignment of display-formatted values
@end itemize

@strong{Examples:}

@example
" Simple variable assignment "
x := 10.
y := x + 5.

" Assignment with binary literal "
mask := 0b11110000.
flags := 0b00001111.

" Assignment with decimal "
pi := 3.14159.
radius := 2.5.

" Chained assignment "
a := b := c := 42.

" Assignment with arithmetic expression "
result := (10 + 5) * 2.

" Assignment with method call result "
playerPos := player position.

" Assignment in conditional "
(x := calculateValue) > 100 ifTrue: [
  self handleLarge.
].
@end example

@strong{Paradigm Explanation:}

Assignment in Smalltalk uses `:=` rather than `=` to distinguish it from equality comparison. In Smalltalk, `=` sends an equality message, while `:=` is a language-level assignment operator. Variables are initially undefined and become bound through assignment. Smalltalk permits chained assignments, where the rightmost value is assigned to all variables from right to left.

@node Smalltalk Return Statements
@subsection Return Statements (Explicit Return)

@strong{Definition:} The return operator (^) explicitly returns a value from a method or block, terminating execution immediately.

@strong{Syntax:}
@example
^@var{expression}.
^@var{value}.
^self.
^nil.
@end example

@strong{Numeric Type Support:}
@itemize
@item BINARY: Returning binary integer values
@item DECIMAL: Returning floating-point values
@item DISPLAY: Returning display-formatted results
@end itemize

@strong{Examples:}

@example
" Return value from method "
getValue
  ^42.

" Return expression result "
double: number
  ^number * 2.

" Return binary calculation result "
getMask
  ^0b1111 + 0b0001.

" Early return from conditional "
validate: value
  (value < 0) ifTrue: [^false].
  (value > 100) ifTrue: [^false].
  ^true.

" Return block value "
compute
  result := [| x |
    x := 10.
    ^x * x.
  ] value.
  ^result.

" Return self for method chaining "
show
  Transcript show: self.
  ^self.
@end example

@strong{Paradigm Explanation:}

Return statements in Smalltalk are implemented as exceptions that unwind the stack to the method's caller. The explicit return (^) provides an alternative to implicit return of the last expression. In blocks, an explicit return returns from the enclosing method, not just the block---this behavior differs from languages where return from a lambda returns only from the lambda. This design ensures that blocks maintain their semantic connection to the enclosing method.

@node Smalltalk Arithmetic Operations
@subsection Arithmetic Operations

@strong{Definition:} Arithmetic operations perform mathematical calculations on numeric values. Smalltalk supports standard binary operators: +, -, *, /, //, and \.

@strong{Syntax:}
@example
@var{number1} + @var{number2}.
@var{number1} - @var{number2}.
@var{number1} * @var{number2}.
@var{number1} / @var{number2}.
@var{number1} // @var{number2}.
@var{number1} \\ @var{number2}.
@end example

@strong{Numeric Type Support:}
@itemize
@item BINARY: Integer arithmetic with coercion
@item DECIMAL: Floating-point arithmetic
@item DISPLAY: Integer display operations
@end itemize

@strong{Examples:}

@example
" Basic arithmetic "
sum := 10 + 5.
difference := 20 - 7.
product := 6 * 8.
quotient := 20 / 4.

" Binary operations "
result1 := 0b1010 + 0b0101.
result2 := 0b1111 * 0b0010.
result3 := 0b11111111 // 0b00010000.

" Decimal arithmetic "
area := 3.14 * radius * radius.
average := (score1 + score2) / 2.0.

" Mixed binary and unary operations "
calculated := (0b1000 + 0b0100) * 2.

" Integer division vs. regular division "
intResult := 17 // 5.
floatResult := 17 / 5.

" Modulo operation "
remainder := 17 \\ 5.
@end example

@strong{Paradigm Explanation:}

Arithmetic in Smalltalk uses binary message sends: the + operator is a binary message to the left operand. This design treats arithmetic uniformly with other operations, maintaining object-oriented consistency. Integer division (//) produces integer results; regular division (/) produces floating-point results or rationals. Modulo (\) computes the remainder. Smalltalk's numeric coercion automatically promotes integers to floats when necessary for mixed-type operations.

@node Smalltalk Collections
@subsection Collections (Arrays, OrderedCollections, Dictionaries)

@strong{Definition:} Collections are objects containing multiple elements. Common collections include arrays (fixed-size, index-based with literal notation), ordered collections (resizable sequences), and dictionaries (key-value mappings). Collections are fundamental to managing complex data in Smalltalk, enabling aggregation of related data and homogeneous storage of elements. Smalltalk's collection protocol provides a unified interface across different collection types through polymorphism.

@strong{Syntax:}
@example
#(element1 element2 element3).
Array with: element1 with: element2.
OrderedCollection new.
Dictionary new.
@var{collection} at: index.
@var{collection} at: index put: value.
@var{collection} add: element.
@end example

@strong{Numeric Type Support:}
@itemize
@item BINARY: Arrays of binary integers, collections indexed by binary values
@item DECIMAL: Collections of floating-point numbers for scientific data
@item DISPLAY: Mixed collections with display values and symbolic elements
@end itemize

@strong{Examples:}

@example
" Array literal - read-only compile-time collection "
colors := #(red green blue).

" Dynamic array creation "
numbers := Array with: 1 with: 2 with: 3.

" Accessing array elements by index "
first := colors at: 1.
colors at: 2 put: yellow.

" Array of binary values for flags "
masks := #(0b0001 0b0010 0b0100 0b1000).
mask1 := masks at: 1.
result := value bitAnd: mask1.

" OrderedCollection - mutable resizable sequence "
dynamicList := OrderedCollection new.
dynamicList add: 10.
dynamicList add: 20.
dynamicList add: 30.
dynamicList remove: 20.

" Dictionary - key-value storage "
scores := Dictionary new.
scores at: #player1 put: 100.
scores at: #player2 put: 95.
score := scores at: #player1.

" Decimal array for coordinates "
coordinates := #(1.5 2.3 3.7).
distance := coordinates at: 1.

" Iterating collections "
colors do: [:color |
  Transcript show: color; cr.
].

" Filtering collections with select "
large := numbers select: [:n | n > 50].

" Mapping collections with collect "
doubled := numbers collect: [:n | n * 2].

" Multi-dimensional arrays "
grid := Array new: 10.
grid at: 1 put: (Array new: 10).
(grid at: 1) at: 1 put: 42.

" Nested collections with mixed types "
data := Array with: #names with: (Array with: 'Alice' with: 'Bob').
data at: 1.  " Returns #names "
(data at: 2) at: 1.  " Returns 'Alice' "
@end example

@strong{Paradigm Explanation:}

Collections in Smalltalk are objects that implement a standard protocol (at:, at:put:, add:, do:, select:, collect:, etc.). Arrays use hash-literal notation (#(...)) for immutable compile-time collections; dictionaries use key-value pairs. Collections support iteration through the @code{do:} message, which passes each element to a block. Higher-order operations like @code{select:} (filtering), @code{collect:} (mapping), and @code{inject:into:} (reduction) treat blocks as first-class behavior parameters, enabling functional-style programming within Smalltalk's object-oriented framework. The protocol-based design enables polymorphism: code written for the Collection protocol works with any collection type that implements the protocol, a powerful abstraction enabling generic algorithms.

@node Smalltalk Iteration
@subsection Iteration Methods (do:, collect:, select:)

@strong{Definition:} Iteration methods apply a block to each element of a collection, enabling functional operations without explicit loops. These methods represent the functional programming paradigm within Smalltalk's object-oriented design. Iteration methods provide safe abstractions over collections, preventing off-by-one errors and enabling optimizations by the runtime system.

@strong{Syntax:}
@example
@var{collection} do: [:element | @var{block}].
@var{collection} collect: [:element | @var{newElement}].
@var{collection} select: [:element | @var{condition}].
@var{collection} reject: [:element | @var{condition}].
@var{collection} inject: @var{initial} into: [:accumulator :element | @var{update}].
@var{collection} detect: [:element | @var{condition}].
@end example

@strong{Numeric Type Support:}
@itemize
@item BINARY: Iterating collections of binary integers, bitwise filtering
@item DECIMAL: Iterating floating-point collections for mathematical operations
@item DISPLAY: Iterating mixed numeric types for display and formatting
@end itemize

@strong{Examples:}

@example
" Basic iteration with side effects "
#(1 2 3 4 5) do: [:n |
  Transcript show: n; cr.
].

" Mapping with collect - transform elements "
numbers := #(1 2 3 4 5).
doubled := numbers collect: [:n | n * 2].
" Result: #(2 4 6 8 10) "

" Filtering with select - keep matching elements "
evenNumbers := numbers select: [:n | n \\ 2 = 0].
" Result: #(2 4) "

" Filtering with reject - remove matching elements "
oddNumbers := numbers reject: [:n | n \\ 2 = 0].
" Result: #(1 3 5) "

" Reduction with inject:into: - accumulate result "
sum := #(1 2 3 4 5) inject: 0 into: [:sum :n |
  sum + n.
].
" Result: 15 "

" Finding first matching element "
firstEven := #(1 2 3 4 5) detect: [:n | n \\ 2 = 0].
" Result: 2 "

" Binary collection iteration "
masks := #(0b0001 0b0010 0b0100 0b1000).
masks do: [:mask |
  self applyMask: mask.
].

" Decimal number operations with collect "
decimals := #(1.5 2.3 3.7).
scaled := decimals collect: [:d | d * 10].
" Result: #(15.0 23.0 37.0) "

" Complex iteration - matrix multiplication "
pairs := #((1 10) (2 20) (3 30)).
products := pairs collect: [:pair |
  (pair at: 1) * (pair at: 2).
].
" Result: #(10 40 90) "

" Nested iteration "
matrix := #((1 2 3) (4 5 6) (7 8 9)).
flattened := matrix inject: #() into: [:flat :row |
  flat, row.
].
" Result: #(1 2 3 4 5 6 7 8 9) "

" Iteration with state preservation "
| words count |
words := #('apple' 'banana' 'cherry').
count := 0.
words do: [:word |
  count := count + 1.
  Transcript show: count; show: ': '; show: word; cr.
].
@end example

@strong{Paradigm Explanation:}

Iteration methods implement common patterns without explicit loop constructs. The @code{do:} method provides basic enumeration for side effects; @code{collect:} transforms each element (mapping); @code{select:} filters elements keeping those matching a condition (predicate filtering); @code{reject:} filters keeping those not matching; @code{inject:into:} accumulates a result across all elements (reduction/fold); @code{detect:} finds the first matching element. These methods take blocks as arguments, enabling composition of iteration patterns. The functional approach supports declarative programming: code specifies what to compute, not how. Each method has specific semantics and efficiency characteristics: @code{do:} may exit early if a block returns non-nil; @code{select:} may abort early in some implementations; @code{inject:into:} must process all elements. Runtime systems can optimize these patterns using techniques like loop unrolling, vectorization, or special-casing for known collection types.

@node Smalltalk Reflection
@subsection Reflection and Metaprogramming

@strong{Definition:} Reflection enables programs to inspect and manipulate their own structure, including classes, methods, and instance variables.

@strong{Syntax:}
@example
@var{object} class.
@var{object} respondsTo: #methodName.
@var{class} methodDict.
@var{object} perform: #methodName.
@var{object} perform: #methodName: with: argument.
@end example

@strong{Numeric Type Support:}
@itemize
@item BINARY: Reflection on binary-typed objects
@item DECIMAL: Reflection on floating-point objects
@item DISPLAY: Inspecting numeric display representations
@end itemize

@strong{Examples:}

@example
" Get object's class "
obj := Player new.
objClass := obj class.

" Check method existence "
(game respondsTo: #start) ifTrue: [
  game start.
].

" Perform method dynamically "
selector := #heal:.
player perform: selector with: 10.

" Inspect class methods "
methodDict := Player methodDict.

" Conditional method invocation "
(entity respondsTo: #takeDamage:) ifTrue: [
  entity perform: #takeDamage: with: damage.
].

" Reflection on numeric values "
num := 42.
numClass := num class.
(numClass = SmallInteger) ifTrue: [
  self displayAs: #integer.
].

" Instance variable inspection "
vars := Player instanceVariableNames.
@end example

@strong{Paradigm Explanation:}

Reflection in Smalltalk treats classes and methods as first-class objects, enabling runtime inspection and invocation. The perform: method dynamically sends a message based on a selector symbol, enabling late binding even when the exact method is unknown at compile time. respondsTo: checks whether an object understands a given message. This metaprogramming capability allows for generic algorithms and frameworks that adapt to different object structures.

@node Muddle Forms and Constructs
@section Muddle Forms and Constructs

Muddle (Macro Utility Definition Language Environment) is a Lisp-descended dynamically-typed language with mutable data structures, control structures for handling failures and non-local exits, and an extensive system of evaluation strategies. This section documents core Muddle forms.

@node Muddle SETG
@subsection SETG (Global Variable Assignment)

@strong{Definition:} SETG establishes or updates a global variable binding, creating persistent storage for a value in the global environment. Unlike local variables (SETL) which exist only within their enclosing function, global variables persist across function boundaries and remain accessible throughout the program execution. SETG is fundamental to creating shared mutable state in Muddle programs.

@strong{Syntax:}
@example
<SETG atom value>
<SETG atom <expression>>
<SETG var1 val1 var2 val2 ...>
@end example

@strong{Numeric Type Support:}
@itemize
@item BINARY: Global variables holding binary integer values with bitwise operations
@item DECIMAL: Global variables storing decimal/floating-point values used in calculations
@item DISPLAY: Global variables storing display-formatted values or symbols for display modes
@end itemize

@strong{Examples:}

@example
" Simple global assignment - creates global variable "
<SETG COUNTER 0>

" Global with arithmetic expression "
<SETG TOTAL <+ 100 50>>

" Binary global variable "
<SETG MASK 0b11111111>

" Multiple assignments in single SETG "
<SETG X 10 Y 20 Z 30>

" Global with decimal value "
<SETG PI 3.14159>

" Update existing global by referencing it "
<SETG COUNTER <+ .COUNTER 1>>

" Global with result of function call "
<SETG PLAYER <MAKE-PLAYER "Hero" 100>>

" Global containing list structure "
<SETG SCORES <LIST 100 200 150>>

" Global tracking state across function calls "
<SETG STATE 'initialized>
<DEFUN CHANGE-STATE (NEW-STATE)
  <SETG STATE .NEW-STATE>>

" Binary operations on global "
<SETG FLAGS 0b00000000>
<SETG FLAGS <BITOR .FLAGS 0b00000001>>
@end example

@strong{Paradigm Explanation:}

SETG is Muddle's mechanism for creating and modifying persistent shared state. In Lisp tradition, global variables are distinguished by asterisks (*var*) by convention, though Muddle uses the .VAR dot notation for access in many contexts. SETG creates an unconditional binding: if the variable already exists, SETG overwrites it; if it doesn't exist, SETG creates it. Multiple variables can be assigned in a single SETG form, evaluated left to right. The value assigned is the evaluated expression result. SETG is procedurally oriented, fundamentally different from functional approaches; in purely functional code, global state modification via SETG is avoided in favor of explicit parameter passing. However, SETG is essential for implementing stateful algorithms, managing configuration, and creating caches in Muddle.

@node Muddle SETL
@subsection SETL (Local Variable Assignment)

@strong{Definition:} SETL creates or updates a local variable within the current lexical scope, affecting only the current execution context.

@strong{Syntax:}
@example
<SETL atom value>
<SETL atom <expression>>
<SETL var1 val1 var2 val2>
@end example

@strong{Numeric Type Support:}
@itemize
@item BINARY: Local variables containing binary integers
@item DECIMAL: Local variables with floating-point values
@item DISPLAY: Local variables storing display data
@end itemize

@strong{Examples:}

@example
" Local variable in a function "
<DEFUN CALCULATE ()
  <SETL X 10>
  <SETL Y 20>
  <+ .X .Y>
>

" Local with binary operations "
<SETL MASK 0b1010>
<SETL VALUE 0b1111>

" Multiple local assignments "
<SETL A 1 B 2 C 3>

" Local variable shadowing "
<DEFUN OUTER ()
  <SETL X 100>
  <DEFUN INNER ()
    <SETL X 200>
    .X
  >
  <INNER>
>

" Local with decimal "
<SETL RADIUS 2.5>
<SETL AREA <* 3.14 <* .RADIUS .RADIUS>>>
@end example

@strong{Paradigm Explanation:}

SETL differs from SETG in scope---local bindings exist only within the function or block containing the SETL form. Multiple SETLs can bind multiple variables in sequence. Local variables shadow globals with the same name, enabling local variable overrides. Unlike global variables (accessed as .VAR), local variables in Muddle may be accessed directly depending on evaluation context.

@node Muddle DEFINE
@subsection DEFINE (Function Definition)

@strong{Definition:} DEFINE establishes a function, binding a symbol to a callable object (similar to DEFUN in Common Lisp). Functions are fundamental computational units in Muddle, enabling abstraction, code reuse, and modular program structure. DEFINE creates function objects that can be stored in variables, passed to higher-order functions, and invoked dynamically.

@strong{Syntax:}
@example
<DEFINE symbol parameters body>
<DEFINE (SYMBOL arg1 arg2) <+ arg1 arg2>>
<DEFINE MYFUNCTION (X) <* X X>>
<DEFINE NAME (PARAM1 PARAM2 ...) <function-body>>
@end example

@strong{Numeric Type Support:}
@itemize
@item BINARY: Functions performing binary operations and returning binary results
@item DECIMAL: Functions with decimal arithmetic and floating-point calculations
@item DISPLAY: Functions formatting and displaying numeric output
@end itemize

@strong{Examples:}

@example
" Simple function definition "
<DEFINE SQUARE (X) <* X X>>

" Function with multiple parameters "
<DEFINE ADD-THREE (A B C) <+ A <+ B C>>>

" Binary operations in function "
<DEFINE BIT-MASK (DATA MASK)
  <BITAND .DATA .MASK>
>

" Function returning expression result "
<DEFINE DOUBLE (X) <* 2 X>>

" Function with binary arithmetic "
<DEFINE OR-BITS (A B)
  <BITOR .A .B>
>

" Decimal calculation function "
<DEFINE CIRCLE-AREA (RADIUS)
  <* 3.14159 <* .RADIUS .RADIUS>>
>

" Function calling other functions "
<DEFINE HYPOTENUSE (A B)
  <SQRT <+ <* .A .A> <* .B .B>>>
>

" Recursive function "
<DEFINE FACTORIAL (N)
  <COND
    ((= .N 0) 1)
    (T <* .N <FACTORIAL <-  .N 1>>>>
  >
>

" Function with local variables "
<DEFINE SAFE-DIVIDE (A B)
  <PROG (RESULT)
    <IF (= .B 0)
        <SETL RESULT 'error>
        <SETL RESULT </ .A .B>>
    >
    .RESULT
  >
>

" Function with complex control flow "
<DEFINE PROCESS-VALUE (X)
  <COND
    ((< .X 0) <SETG STATE 'negative>)
    ((= .X 0) <SETG STATE 'zero>)
    ((> .X 100) <SETG STATE 'large>)
    (T <SETG STATE 'normal>)
  >
  .STATE
>

" Function computing with LOOP "
<DEFINE SUM-RANGE (START END)
  <PROG (RESULT)
    <SETL RESULT 0>
    <LOOP ((I .START))
          (<= .I .END)
          ((SETL I <+ .I 1>))
          <SETL RESULT <+ .RESULT .I>>
    >
    .RESULT
  >
>
@end example

@strong{Paradigm Explanation:}

DEFINE in Muddle binds a name to a function object in the global environment. Parameters are specified in a list; within the function body, parameters are accessed with dot notation (.PARAM). The function body is an expression that returns a value---the last expression evaluated becomes the function's result. DEFINE is globally scoped, creating persistent function bindings, though it can also be used locally within PROG blocks to create locally-scoped functions. Functions in Muddle are first-class objects: they can be stored in variables (SETG, SETL), passed as arguments to higher-order functions, returned from functions, and invoked dynamically via CALL or FUNCALL. This first-class status enables functional programming patterns like function composition, currying, and callbacks. Recursive functions are supported naturally; the function can call itself by name. Functions can be interactive, taking input and producing output, or pure, computing results without side effects.

@node Muddle COND
@subsection COND (Multi-Way Branching)

@strong{Definition:} COND provides multi-way conditional branching with multiple test-action pairs evaluated sequentially until one test succeeds. Unlike simple IF with two branches, COND elegantly handles multiple conditions without nested IF-ELSE chains, improving readability and maintainability. COND is fundamental to structured programming in Muddle and Lisp-family languages.

@strong{Syntax:}
@example
<COND (test1 action1) (test2 action2) ... (testN actionN)>
<COND ((.X 100) <SET RESULT 1>)
      ((.X 50) <SET RESULT 2>)
      (T <SET RESULT 3>)>
@end example

@strong{Numeric Type Support:}
@itemize
@item BINARY: Conditional branching on binary value comparisons and flags
@item DECIMAL: Comparisons with decimal numbers for range checks
@item DISPLAY: Display-based conditional logic for user interaction
@end itemize

@strong{Examples:}

@example
" Simple COND with numeric comparisons "
<COND ((.X 10) <SET RESULT 'small>)
      ((.X 50) <SET RESULT 'medium>)
      (T <SET RESULT 'large>)>

" COND with arithmetic comparisons "
<COND ((< .AGE 13) <SET STATUS 'child>)
      ((< .AGE 18) <SET STATUS 'teen>)
      ((< .AGE 65) <SET STATUS 'adult>)
      (T <SET STATUS 'senior>)>

" Binary value COND for flag checking "
<COND ((= .MASK 0b1111) <ACTIVATE-ALL>)
      ((= .MASK 0b1010) <ACTIVATE-EVEN>)
      ((= .MASK 0b0000) <DEACTIVATE-ALL>)
      (T <ACTIVATE-CUSTOM>)>

" Decimal comparison COND for temperature "
<COND ((> .TEMPERATURE 100) <SET STATE 'hot>)
      ((> .TEMPERATURE 32) <SET STATE 'warm>)
      ((> .TEMPERATURE 0) <SET STATE 'cold>)
      (T <SET STATE 'freezing>)>

" Nested COND structures "
<COND ((.X 10)
       <COND ((.Y 5) <SET RESULT 'small-small>)
             ((< .Y 20) <SET RESULT 'small-medium>)
             (T <SET RESULT 'small-large>)>)
      ((< .X 50)
       <COND ((.Y 5) <SET RESULT 'medium-small>)
             (T <SET RESULT 'medium-large>)>)
      (T <SET RESULT 'large>)>

" COND with list/atom testing "
<COND ((<ATOM .DATA> <PROCESS-ATOMIC .DATA>))
      ((<LIST? .DATA> <PROCESS-LIST .DATA>))
      (T <ERROR 'unknown-type>)>

" COND dispatching based on message type "
<COND ((= .MESSAGE-TYPE 'start> <INITIALIZE>)
      ((= .MESSAGE-TYPE 'update> <UPDATE-STATE>)
      ((= .MESSAGE-TYPE 'stop> <SHUTDOWN>)
      (T <PRINT "Unknown message">)>

" COND with complex expressions "
<COND ((<AND (> .X 0) (< .X 10)) <SET CLASS 'small>)
      ((<AND (>= .X 10) (< .X 50)) <SET CLASS 'medium>)
      ((<AND (>= .X 50) (< .X 100)) <SET CLASS 'large>)
      (T <SET CLASS 'huge>)>
@end example

@strong{Paradigm Explanation:}

COND is Muddle's fundamental multi-way conditional construct. It evaluates test-action pairs (clauses) sequentially from top to bottom. The first clause whose test evaluates to true (non-NIL, non-zero, non-false) causes its associated action to execute; subsequent clauses are skipped. COND returns the value of the executed action clause. Using @code{T} (the true literal) as the final test's condition creates a default clause that always executes if all previous tests failed, ensuring at least one branch executes. This catch-all default pattern enhances robustness by handling unexpected cases. COND is powerful for implementing decision trees and multi-way switches more readably than nested IF-ELSE-IF chains. Each clause is independently readable, and new clauses can be added without restructuring existing code. COND clauses can contain arbitrary Muddle code, enabling complex actions including loops, function calls, and nested COND structures.

@node Muddle IF
@subsection IF (Simple Conditional)

@strong{Definition:} IF is a simple three-way conditional: if condition is true, execute the then-clause; otherwise, execute the else-clause.

@strong{Syntax:}
@example
<IF condition then-clause else-clause>
<IF (> .X 10) <PRINT "X is large"> <PRINT "X is small">>
@end example

@strong{Numeric Type Support:}
@itemize
@item BINARY: IF conditions on binary comparison
@item DECIMAL: IF conditions on floating-point values
@item DISPLAY: Display-conditional IF statements
@end itemize

@strong{Examples:}

@example
" Simple IF "
<IF (> .X 0) <SET SIGN 'positive> <SET SIGN 'negative>>

" IF with binary comparison "
<IF (= <BITAND .FLAGS 0b0001> 0b0001)
    <ACTIVATE-FEATURE-1>
    <DISABLE-FEATURE-1>>

" IF with decimal comparison "
<IF (> .TEMPERATURE 98.6) <TRIGGER-ALARM> <CLEAR-ALARM>>

" Nested IF "
<IF (> .X 0)
    <IF (> .X 100) <SET CLASS 'large> <SET CLASS 'medium>>
    <SET CLASS 'negative>>

" IF without else (returns NIL if condition is false) "
<IF (= .STATUS 'ready) <START-PROCESS>>

" IF with function calls "
<IF <VALID-INPUT? .INPUT>
    <PROCESS-INPUT .INPUT>
    <SHOW-ERROR "Invalid input">>
@end example

@strong{Paradigm Explanation:}

IF is simpler than COND, supporting only two branches. It returns the value of the executed branch. If the else-clause is omitted, IF returns NIL if the condition is false. IF differs from COND in readability for binary decisions; COND suits multi-way branches. Both are fundamental control structures in Muddle.

@node Muddle REPEAT
@subsection REPEAT (Indefinite Loop)

@strong{Definition:} REPEAT is an infinite loop construct that repeatedly executes a body until explicitly exited via RETURN or a non-local exit.

@strong{Syntax:}
@example
<REPEAT body>
<REPEAT <PROGN (SETL I 0) (LOOP ...)>>
@end example

@strong{Numeric Type Support:}
@itemize
@item BINARY: REPEATs with binary loop counters
@item DECIMAL: REPEATs with decimal arithmetic
@item DISPLAY: REPEATs for repeated display operations
@end itemize

@strong{Examples:}

@example
" Infinite loop with explicit exit "
<REPEAT
  <PRINT "Counting...">
  <SETL COUNT <+ .COUNT 1>>
  <IF (> .COUNT 10) <RETURN .COUNT>>
>

" REPEAT with accumulation "
<REPEAT
  <SETL VALUE <RANDOM 100>>
  <SETL SUM <+ .SUM .VALUE>>
  <IF (> .SUM 500) <RETURN .SUM>>
>

" Binary counter REPEAT "
<REPEAT
  <SETL MASK <BITASL .MASK 1>>
  <IF (= .MASK 0b100000000) <RETURN>>
>

" REPEAT with nested conditions "
<REPEAT
  <PRINT "Enter value: ">
  <SETL INPUT <READ>>
  <IF (< .INPUT 0)
      <PRINT "Negative!">
      <RETURN .INPUT>>
>
@end example

@strong{Paradigm Explanation:}

REPEAT creates an unbounded loop that continues indefinitely until interrupted by RETURN or an exception (THROW). It serves as the basis for more complex loop constructs. Unlike counted loops (LOOP), REPEAT doesn't manage counters; the programmer is responsible for establishing exit conditions. REPEAT provides a low-level looping mechanism when custom loop logic is necessary.

@node Muddle LOOP
@subsection LOOP (Counted and Conditional Loop)

@strong{Definition:} LOOP creates a structured loop with explicit initialization, condition, and update expressions, providing a more disciplined looping mechanism than REPEAT. LOOP is similar to C's for-loop or Python's range-based loops, enabling clear loop intent and reducing off-by-one errors. LOOP manages loop variables automatically, providing safe and readable iteration.

@strong{Syntax:}
@example
<LOOP initialization condition update body>
<LOOP ((I 1)) (< .I 10) ((SETL I <+ .I 1>)) (PRINT .I)>
<LOOP ((VAR initial-value)) (termination-test) ((update-expr)) body-statements)>
@end example

@strong{Numeric Type Support:}
@itemize
@item BINARY: LOOP with binary iteration counts and bit operations
@item DECIMAL: LOOP with decimal arithmetic operations and floating-point steps
@item DISPLAY: LOOP with display formatting per iteration
@end itemize

@strong{Examples:}

@example
" Simple counted loop from 1 to 10 "
<LOOP ((I 1))
      (< .I 11)
      ((SETL I <+ .I 1>))
      <PRINT .I>
>

" Binary iteration LOOP with bit shifting "
<LOOP ((MASK 0b0001))
      (~= .MASK 0b10000)
      ((SETL MASK <BITASL .MASK 1>))
      <APPLY-MASK .MASK>
>

" Decimal arithmetic LOOP with floating-point step "
<LOOP ((X 0.0))
      (< .X 10.0)
      ((SETL X <+ .X 0.1>))
      <PRINT .X>
>

" Nested loops - multiplication table "
<LOOP ((I 1))
      (<= .I 5)
      ((SETL I <+ .I 1>))
      <LOOP ((J 1))
            (<= .J 5)
            ((SETL J <+ .J 1>))
            <PRINT <* .I .J>>
      >
>

" LOOP with early termination via RETURN "
<PROG (RESULT)
  <LOOP ((I 1))
        (T)
        ((SETL I <+ .I 1>))
        <IF (> .I 5) <RETURN .I>>
  >
  <RETURN 0>
>

" Accumulation pattern in loop "
<PROG (SUM COUNT AVERAGE)
  <SETL SUM 0>
  <SETL COUNT 0>
  <LOOP ((I 1))
        (<= .I 100)
        ((SETL I <+ .I 1>))
        <PROGN
          <SETL SUM <+ .SUM .I>>
          <SETL COUNT <+ .COUNT 1>>
        >
  >
  <SETL AVERAGE </ .SUM .COUNT>>
  .AVERAGE
>

" LOOP with complex update expression "
<LOOP ((VALUE 100) (STEP 10))
      (> .VALUE 0)
      ((SETL VALUE <-  .VALUE .STEP>))
      <PROCESS .VALUE>
>

" Reading list elements with loop counter "
<PROG (LIST)
  <SETL LIST <LIST 10 20 30 40 50>>
  <LOOP ((INDEX 1))
        (<= .INDEX 5)
        ((SETL INDEX <+ .INDEX 1>))
        <PROCESS <NTH .INDEX .LIST>>
  >
>
@end example

@strong{Paradigm Explanation:}

LOOP provides structured iteration combining initialization, condition testing, and update in a single form. The initialization expression(s) establish loop variables; the condition is checked before each iteration; the update expression modifies loop state; the body is executed if the condition is true. Loop variables are local to the LOOP; they cease to exist after the loop terminates. LOOP continues until the condition becomes false or NIL. This structure eliminates common loop bugs: the loop counter is automatically incremented, making off-by-one errors less likely. Multiple loop variables can be initialized and updated, enabling complex iteration patterns like nested loops or loops over multiple sequences simultaneously. LOOP is more readable than REPEAT with manual condition checking and update, particularly for standard iteration patterns like counting and range iteration. LOOP body can contain arbitrary Muddle code, enabling complex loop bodies and early termination via RETURN.

@node Muddle PROG
@subsection PROG (Sequential Evaluation with Local Scope)

@strong{Definition:} PROG sequentially executes forms within a local scope, optionally with local variable declarations. PROG enables multi-statement procedures and serves as the foundation for complex control flow. Unlike functions (DEFINE), PROG creates anonymous procedural blocks inline, perfect for encapsulating algorithms without defining new functions.

@strong{Syntax:}
@example
<PROG (var1 var2 ...) form1 form2 ...>
<PROG (X Y) <SETL X 10> <SETL Y 20> <+ .X .Y>>
<PROG (LOCAL-VARS) init-statement ... final-expression>
@end example

@strong{Numeric Type Support:}
@itemize
@item BINARY: PROG with binary variable bindings and operations
@item DECIMAL: PROG with decimal arithmetic and floating-point operations
@item DISPLAY: PROG with display operations and output formatting
@end itemize

@strong{Examples:}

@example
" PROG with local variables "
<PROG (X Y)
  <SETL X 10>
  <SETL Y 20>
  <+ .X .Y>
>

" PROG returning a value "
<PROG (RESULT)
  <SETL RESULT 0>
  <SETL RESULT <+ .RESULT 100>>
  .RESULT
>

" Binary PROG for bit manipulation "
<PROG (MASK COUNT)
  <SETL MASK 0b00000001>
  <SETL COUNT 0>
  <LOOP ((I 1))
        (<= .I 8)
        ((SETL I <+ .I 1>))
        <PROGN
          <SETL MASK <BITASL .MASK 1>>
          <SETL COUNT <+ .COUNT 1>>
        >
  >
  .MASK
>

" Decimal PROG for calculations "
<PROG (RADIUS AREA CIRCUMFERENCE)
  <SETL RADIUS 5.0>
  <SETL AREA <* 3.14159 <* .RADIUS .RADIUS>>>
  <SETL CIRCUMFERENCE <* 2.0 <* 3.14159 .RADIUS>>>
  <LIST .AREA .CIRCUMFERENCE>
>

" PROG with nested structure and conditionals "
<PROG (STATE INPUT RESULT)
  <SETL STATE 'ready>
  <SETL INPUT <READ>>
  <IF (<= .INPUT 0)
      <PROGN <SETL RESULT 'invalid> <SETG ERROR-CODE 1>)
      <PROGN <SETL RESULT 'valid> <SETG ERROR-CODE 0>)>
  .RESULT
>

" PROG with multiple steps and state management "
<PROG (COUNTER SUM AVERAGE)
  <SETL COUNTER 0>
  <SETL SUM 0>
  <REPEAT
    <SETL VALUE <READ>>
    <IF (= .VALUE -1) <RETURN>)
    <SETL SUM <+ .SUM .VALUE>>
    <SETL COUNTER <+ .COUNTER 1>>
  >
  <SETL AVERAGE </ .SUM .COUNTER>>
  .AVERAGE
>

" PROG with error handling "
<PROG (RESULT ERROR)
  <IF <VALID-INPUT? .DATA>
      <SETL RESULT <PROCESS .DATA>>
      <PROGN
        <SETL ERROR 'invalid-input>
        <SETL RESULT NIL>
      >
  >
  <COND
    (.ERROR <PRINT .ERROR>)
    (.RESULT <PRINT "Success: " .RESULT>)
    (T <PRINT "Unknown error">)
  >
>

" PROG combining multiple loop patterns "
<PROG (TOTAL COUNT AVERAGE MIN MAX)
  <SETL TOTAL 0>
  <SETL COUNT 0>
  <SETL MIN 999999>
  <SETL MAX -999999>
  <LOOP ((I 1))
        (<= .I 100)
        ((SETL I <+ .I 1>))
        <PROGN
          <SETL VALUE <READ>>
          <SETL TOTAL <+ .TOTAL .VALUE>>
          <SETL COUNT <+ .COUNT 1>>
          <IF (< .VALUE .MIN) <SETL MIN .VALUE>)
          <IF (> .VALUE .MAX) <SETL MAX .VALUE>)
        >
  >
  <SETL AVERAGE </ .TOTAL .COUNT>>
  <LIST .AVERAGE .MIN .MAX>
>
@end example

@strong{Paradigm Explanation:}

PROG creates a local scope where variables can be declared and initialized. The first argument is a list of variable names; subsequent arguments are expressions evaluated sequentially in that scope. Local variables are initialized to NIL automatically; SETL can assign values. PROG returns the value of the last expression evaluated. This structure enables writing small procedures without defining full functions, useful for one-off computations. Variables declared in PROG are local to that PROG; they're inaccessible outside and don't interfere with global variables. PROG provides a clean way to organize multi-step computations and enable loops with complex logic. Combined with RETURN, PROG can exit early, bypassing remaining code. Multiple PROG forms can be nested, creating nested scopes; inner PROG variables shadow outer ones.

@node Muddle RETURN
@subsection RETURN (Non-local Exit with Value)

@strong{Definition:} RETURN exits the current PROG block, returning a specified value to the caller of that PROG.

@strong{Syntax:}
@example
<RETURN value>
<RETURN expression>
<RETURN (+ 1 2)>
@end example

@strong{Numeric Type Support:}
@itemize
@item BINARY: RETURN with binary integer values
@item DECIMAL: RETURN with floating-point values
@item DISPLAY: RETURN with display-formatted results
@end itemize

@strong{Examples:}

@example
" Return constant "
<RETURN 42>

" Return expression result "
<RETURN <+ 10 20>>

" Return binary value "
<RETURN 0b11111111>

" Return decimal "
<RETURN 3.14>

" Return from nested loop "
<PROG (RESULT)
  <LOOP ((I 1)) (< .I 100) ((SETL I <+ .I 1>))
    <IF (> .I 50) <RETURN .I>>
  >
  <RETURN 0>
>

" Return multiple values (via list) "
<RETURN <LIST 100 200 300>>

" Conditional RETURN "
<PROG (VALUE)
  <SETL VALUE <READ>>
  <IF (< .VALUE 0) <RETURN 'negative>>
  <IF (= .VALUE 0) <RETURN 'zero>>
  <RETURN 'positive>
>
@end example

@strong{Paradigm Explanation:}

RETURN provides non-local exit from a PROG block, bypassing remaining code and returning directly to the PROG's caller. This enables early termination from loops or nested structures. RETURN is semantically different from a simple expression; it's a control transfer mechanism. Combined with condition checks, RETURN enables efficient handling of exceptional cases without nested conditionals.

@node Muddle CALL
@subsection CALL (Function Application)

@strong{Definition:} CALL invokes a function with specified arguments, evaluating the function in the current environment.

@strong{Syntax:}
@example
<CALL function arg1 arg2 ...>
<CALL .MYFUNC 10 20>
<CALL <COND ...> value>
@end example

@strong{Numeric Type Support:}
@itemize
@item BINARY: Calling functions with binary arguments
@item DECIMAL: Calling functions with decimal values
@item DISPLAY: Calling display-related functions
@end itemize

@strong{Examples:}

@example
" Simple function call "
<CALL SQUARE 5>

" Binary argument CALL "
<CALL BIT-AND 0b1111 0b1010>

" Decimal argument CALL "
<CALL SQRT 16.0>

" CALL with computed function "
<CALL <IF (> .X 0) POSITIVE-HANDLER NEGATIVE-HANDLER> .X>

" CALL within CALL "
<CALL COMBINE
  <CALL TRANSFORM .A>
  <CALL TRANSFORM .B>>

" CALL with multiple returns "
<CALL MULTI-VALUE-FUNC 10 20>
@end example

@strong{Paradigm Explanation:}

CALL is explicit function application. While normal Muddle code uses parentheses for function calls (equivalent to FUNCALL in Lisp), CALL provides alternative syntax. CALL permits the function position to contain any expression that evaluates to a function, enabling higher-order programming. This reflects Muddle's treatment of functions as first-class values.

@node Muddle EVAL
@subsection EVAL (Evaluation at Runtime)

@strong{Definition:} EVAL evaluates an expression at runtime, enabling meta-programming where code is generated or modified dynamically.

@strong{Syntax:}
@example
<EVAL expression>
<EVAL form>
<EVAL <LIST 'SETL 'X 10>>
@end example

@strong{Numeric Type Support:}
@itemize
@item BINARY: EVALing expressions with binary values
@item DECIMAL: EVALing decimal arithmetic expressions
@item DISPLAY: EVALing display-generating code
@end itemize

@strong{Examples:}

@example
" Simple EVAL "
<EVAL <QUOTE <+ 10 20>>>

" EVAL with constructed form "
<EVAL <LIST '+ 5 3>>

" EVAL with binary operations "
<EVAL <LIST 'BITAND 0b1111 0b1010>>

" EVAL with conditionals "
<EVAL <IF (> .X 10)
          <QUOTE <SETL RESULT 'big>>
          <QUOTE <SETL RESULT 'small>>>>

" EVAL generating variables "
<PROG (VAR-NAME)
  <SETL VAR-NAME 'MY-VARIABLE>
  <EVAL <LIST 'SETL .VAR-NAME 100>>
>

" EVAL with decimal expression "
<EVAL <LIST '* 3.14159 <LIST '* 5.0 5.0>>>

" Macro expansion via EVAL "
<EVAL <EXPAND-MACRO MY-MACRO-CALL>>
@end example

@strong{Paradigm Explanation:}

EVAL is Muddle's reflection and meta-programming mechanism, evaluating an expression at runtime in the current environment. Unlike compile-time evaluation, EVAL defers evaluation until runtime, enabling dynamic code generation and macro expansion. EVAL respects lexical scope, accessing local and global variables from its calling context. This feature enables powerful but complex programming patterns and is often used for domain-specific languages or configuration scripts.

@node Muddle QUOTE
@subsection QUOTE (Prevent Evaluation)

@strong{Definition:} QUOTE returns its argument unevaluated as a literal value, preventing automatic evaluation.

@strong{Syntax:}
@example
'@var{expression}
<QUOTE @var{expression}>
@end example

@strong{Numeric Type Support:}
@itemize
@item BINARY: Quoting binary literal values
@item DECIMAL: Quoting decimal literals
@item DISPLAY: Quoting symbolic display values
@end itemize

@strong{Examples:}

@example
" Quote symbol "
'MYVARIABLE

" Quote function call form (returns form, not result) "
'(+ 1 2)

" Quote list "
'(A B C)

" Quote binary literal "
'0b11111111

" Quote for symbolic data "
'(ACTION MOVE DESTINATION (10 20))

" Quoting in data structure "
(LIST 'OPCODE 'ADD 'RESULT 'A)

" Quote for code generation "
(LIST 'SETL 'X '100)
@end example

@strong{Paradigm Explanation:}

QUOTE is fundamental to Lisp and Muddle, enabling symbolic data processing. Quoted expressions are returned as s-expressions without evaluation. This distinction between code (evaluated forms) and data (quoted forms) is central to metaprogramming. QUOTE enables construction of code as data, facilitating macros, code generation, and domain-specific languages. The single quote (') is shorthand for QUOTE.

@node Muddle LAMBDA
@subsection LAMBDA (Anonymous Function Creation)

@strong{Definition:} LAMBDA creates an anonymous function object with parameters and a body, without binding it to a global name via DEFINE. LAMBDA enables functional programming patterns, higher-order functions, and dynamic function creation. Lambda functions are first-class objects in Muddle, enabling sophisticated meta-programming and abstraction techniques.

@strong{Syntax:}
@example
<LAMBDA (param1 param2 ...) body>
<LAMBDA (X) <+ .X 1>>
<LAMBDA (A B) <* .A .B>>
<LAMBDA (X Y Z) <+ .X <+ .Y .Z>>>
@end example

@strong{Numeric Type Support:}
@itemize
@item BINARY: LAMBDA functions performing binary operations and returning binary results
@item DECIMAL: LAMBDA functions with floating-point operations and transcendental functions
@item DISPLAY: LAMBDA functions for display operations and string formatting
@end itemize

@strong{Examples:}

@example
" Simple LAMBDA - increment function "
<LAMBDA (X) <+ .X 1>>

" LAMBDA with multiple parameters "
<LAMBDA (A B) <+ .A .B>>

" LAMBDA with binary operations "
<LAMBDA (DATA MASK) <BITAND .DATA .MASK>>

" Assigning LAMBDA to variable "
<SETL ADDER <LAMBDA (X Y) <+ .X .Y>>>

" Calling LAMBDA via CALL "
<CALL <LAMBDA (X) <* 2 .X>> 5>

" LAMBDA with decimal operations "
<SETL SCALE <LAMBDA (RADIUS) <* 3.14159 <* .RADIUS .RADIUS>>>>

" LAMBDA passed to higher-order functions "
<MAP <LAMBDA (X) <+ .X 1>> '(1 2 3 4 5))>

" LAMBDA with closure capturing enclosing variables "
<PROG (MULTIPLIER)
  <SETL MULTIPLIER 10>
  <SETL MULTIPLY <LAMBDA (X) <* .MULTIPLIER .X>>>
  <CALL .MULTIPLY 5>  " Returns 50 "
>

" LAMBDA composed with other functions "
<SETL DOUBLE <LAMBDA (X) <* 2 .X>>>
<SETL INCREMENT <LAMBDA (X) <+ .X 1>>>
<CALL .DOUBLE <CALL .INCREMENT 5>>
" Calls (increment 5) = 6, then (double 6) = 12 "

" LAMBDA with complex computation "
<SETL SAFE-DIVIDE <LAMBDA (A B)
  <IF (= .B 0)
      'error
      </ .A .B>
  >
>>

" LAMBDA as dispatch table entry "
<SETL OPERATORS
  <LIST
    <LAMBDA (A B) <+ .A .B>>
    <LAMBDA (A B) <-  .A .B>>
    <LAMBDA (A B) <* .A .B>>
  >
>

" LAMBDA building arithmetic expressions "
<SETL POLY <LAMBDA (X)
  <+ <* 3 <* .X .X>>
     <+ <* 2 .X> 1>
  >
>>
" Computes 3x^2 + 2x + 1 "

" LAMBDA with conditional logic "
<SETL ABSOLUTE <LAMBDA (X)
  <IF (< .X 0)
      <-  0 .X>
      .X
  >
>>

" Multiple LAMBDA parameters with state "
<PROG (X)
  <SETL X 0>
  <SETL COUNTER <LAMBDA ()
    <PROGN
      <SETL X <+ .X 1>>
      .X
    >
  >>
  <CALL .COUNTER>  " Returns 1 "
  <CALL .COUNTER>  " Returns 2 "
@end example

@strong{Paradigm Explanation:}

LAMBDA creates function objects without a top-level binding, enabling functional programming and higher-order functions. LAMBDA functions capture their definition environment (closures), accessing local and global variables from their creation context. When called via CALL or through higher-order functions like MAP, LAMBDA functions execute with their captured environment. This enables sophisticated patterns: binding variables to lambda-captured values, building function tables, and composing functions. LAMBDA supports anonymous computation---code can create functions dynamically without naming them. In combination with SETL and SETG, LAMBDA enables variable binding to function values, facilitating patterns like strategy selection, algorithm abstraction, and dynamic dispatch. LAMBDA bodies can contain arbitrary Muddle code, including conditionals, loops, nested LAMBDA definitions, and recursion. This makes LAMBDA the foundation of Muddle's functional programming capabilities.

@node Muddle ATOM
@subsection ATOM (Type Testing for Atoms)

@strong{Definition:} ATOM tests whether its argument is an atom (atomic value) versus a structured object like a list.

@strong{Syntax:}
@example
<ATOM object>
<ATOM .VARIABLE>
@end example

@strong{Numeric Type Support:}
@itemize
@item BINARY: Testing if binary values are atoms (they are)
@item DECIMAL: Testing if floats are atoms (they are)
@item DISPLAY: Checking if display values are atomic
@end itemize

@strong{Examples:}

@example
" Testing atoms "
<ATOM 42>          " T (is atom) "
<ATOM 'SYMBOL>     " T (is atom) "
<ATOM 0b1111>      " T (is atom) "
<ATOM 3.14>        " T (is atom) "

" Testing non-atoms "
<ATOM '(A B C)>    " NIL (is list, not atom) "
<ATOM <LIST 1 2>>  " NIL (is list, not atom) "

" Atom test in conditional "
<IF <ATOM .X>
    <PRINT "X is atomic">
    <PRINT "X is a structure">>

" Type dispatch based on atom check "
<COND
  (<ATOM .DATA> <PROCESS-ATOMIC .DATA>)
  (<LIST .DATA> <PROCESS-LIST .DATA>)
  (T <ERROR "Unknown type">) 
>

" Binary atom check "
<ATOM 0b10101010>  " T "

" Decimal atom check "
<ATOM -17.5>       " T "
@end example

@strong{Paradigm Explanation:}

ATOM is a type predicate fundamental to Lisp-family languages. Atoms include numbers, symbols, strings, and other indivisible values. Non-atoms include lists and other composite structures. ATOM enables dynamic type checking and conditional code paths based on data structure type. This supports generic programming where algorithms handle different data types through type-testing predicates.

@node Muddle LIST
@subsection LIST (List Construction)

@strong{Definition:} LIST constructs a list from its arguments, treating each argument as an element and returning a new list containing those elements.

@strong{Syntax:}
@example
<LIST elem1 elem2 elem3 ...>
<LIST value1 value2>
<LIST>
@end example

@strong{Numeric Type Support:}
@itemize
@item BINARY: Lists containing binary integer elements
@item DECIMAL: Lists containing decimal values
@item DISPLAY: Lists of mixed numeric display types
@end itemize

@strong{Examples:}

@example
" Simple list construction "
<LIST 1 2 3 4 5>

" List with symbols "
<LIST 'A 'B 'C>

" Empty list "
<LIST>

" List with binary values "
<LIST 0b0001 0b0010 0b0100 0b1000>

" List with decimal values "
<LIST 1.5 2.3 3.7 4.2>

" Mixed list "
<LIST 'NAME 'Player 'HEALTH 100 'MANA 50>

" List with nested list "
<LIST 'POSITION <LIST 10 20> 'COLOR 'red>

" Constructed list in SETL "
<SETL DATA <LIST 100 200 300>>

" LIST with expression results "
<LIST <+ 10 20> <* 3 4> </ 20 5>>
@end example

@strong{Paradigm Explanation:}

LIST is Muddle's primary list constructor, creating a cons-cell chain representing a list. Unlike quoted lists ('(...)), LIST evaluates all its arguments first, enabling computed list elements. This mirrors Lisp's LIST function. Combined with other list operations (CAR, CDR, APPEND), LIST enables construction and manipulation of structured data. Lists form the basis of Muddle's symbolic processing capabilities.

@node Muddle FUNCTION
@subsection FUNCTION (Reference to Function)

@strong{Definition:} FUNCTION returns a reference to a function, similar to QUOTE for function values, enabling passing functions as first-class objects.

@strong{Syntax:}
@example
#'@var{functionname}
<FUNCTION @var{functionname}>
@end example

@strong{Numeric Type Support:}
@itemize
@item BINARY: Functions operating on binary values
@item DECIMAL: Functions with decimal arithmetic
@item DISPLAY: Display-related functions
@end itemize

@strong{Examples:}

@example
" Get reference to a function "
#'SQUARE

" Using function reference with CALL "
<CALL #'SQUARE 5>

" Passing function to higher-order function "
<MAP #'DOUBLE '(1 2 3 4 5))>

" Storing function reference "
<SETL HANDLER #'PROCESS-EVENT>

" Conditionally selecting function "
<SETL OPERATION
  <IF (= .MODE 'debug)
      #'DEBUG-TRACE
      #'NORMAL-EXECUTE>>

" Anonymous function with LAMBDA "
<SETL TRANSFORM #'(LAMBDA (X) <+ .X 100>)>

" Function reference in data structure "
<SETL OPERATIONS
  <LIST #'INITIALIZE #'UPDATE #'RENDER #'CLEANUP>>
@end example

@strong{Paradigm Explanation:}

FUNCTION enables treating functions as values, essential for higher-order programming. Unlike merely naming a function, FUNCTION creates a reference that can be stored, passed, or selected conditionally. This supports callbacks, dispatch tables, and functional composition. The #' syntax is shorthand for FUNCTION, improving readability. Combined with CALL and higher-order functions, FUNCTION enables functional programming patterns in Muddle.

@node Lingo Forms and Constructs
@section Lingo Forms and Constructs

Lingo is Macromedia Director's scripting language, designed for multimedia and interactive application development. This section documents core Lingo language forms and constructs.

@node Lingo Message Handlers
@subsection Message Handlers (on/end)

@strong{Definition:} Message handlers respond to events or messages, defining behavior triggered by specific events (mouse clicks, keyboard input, frame transitions, etc.).

@strong{Syntax:}
@example
on @var{messageName}
  @var{statements}
end

on @var{messageName} @var{arg1}, @var{arg2}
  @var{statements}
end
@end example

@strong{Numeric Type Support:}
@itemize
@item BINARY: Handlers with binary event flags
@item DECIMAL: Handlers performing decimal calculations
@item DISPLAY: Handlers managing display events
@end itemize

@strong{Examples:}

@example
" Mouse click handler "
on mouseUp
  playSound(#click)
  moveToNextScene()
end

" Mouse down handler with position "
on mouseDown
  spritePos = sprite(me.spriteNum).loc
  updateDisplay(spritePos)
end

" Frame enter handler "
on enterFrame
  updateGameState()
  renderFrame()
end

" Custom message handler "
on objectDestroyed objID
  removeFromList(objID)
  updateScore(100)
end

" Handler with multiple parameters "
on applyDamage damage, source
  health = health - damage
  if health <= 0 then
    die()
  end if
end

" Binary flag handler "
on setFeatureFlags flags
  newMask = bitOr(currentMask, flags)
  applyMask(newMask)
end

" Handler returning value "
on getHealth
  return health
end
@end example

@strong{Paradigm Explanation:}

Message handlers embody Lingo's event-driven architecture. Handlers automatically execute when their corresponding event occurs. Named handlers trigger through explicit message sends; unnamed handlers (like mouseUp) respond to framework events. Handlers can accept parameters passed by their event source. Unlike functions, handlers don't require explicit invocation---the runtime dispatches them based on event occurrence.

@node Lingo Property Access
@subsection Property Access and Manipulation

@strong{Definition:} Properties are object attributes representing state. Lingo provides syntax for reading and modifying properties on sprite objects, cast members, and custom objects.

@strong{Syntax:}
@example
@var{object}.@var{property}
@var{object}.@var{property} = @var{value}
sprite(@var{spriteNum}).@var{property}
member(@var{memberName}).@var{property}
@end example

@strong{Numeric Type Support:}
@itemize
@item BINARY: Binary property values for flags
@item DECIMAL: Decimal properties for coordinates and scaling
@item DISPLAY: Display-related sprite properties
@end itemize

@strong{Examples:}

@example
" Read sprite property "
x = sprite(1).locH
y = sprite(1).locV

" Modify sprite property "
sprite(1).locH = 100
sprite(1).locV = 200

" Cast member property "
text = member('title').text
member('title').text = "New Title"

" Numeric property "
scale = sprite(1).scale
sprite(1).scale = 150

" Binary flag properties "
visible = sprite(1).visible
sprite(1).visible = TRUE

" Decimal coordinate property "
sprite(1).locZ = 10.5
depth = sprite(1).locZ

" Color property (DISPLAY numeric) "
sprite(1).color = rgb(255, 128, 64)

" Property chaining "
owner.parent.properties.value = 42
@end example

@strong{Paradigm Explanation:}

Property access in Lingo uses dot notation, familiar to object-oriented programmers. Properties represent both mutable state and read-only metadata. Sprite properties control display attributes (position, scale, rotation); cast member properties contain the actual media data. The dot notation naturalizes property access as a fundamental operation, distinct from function calls.

@node Lingo Conditionals
@subsection Conditionals (if/then/else)

@strong{Definition:} Conditionals evaluate conditions and execute different code branches based on the result. Lingo uses if-then-else syntax similar to structured languages.

@strong{Syntax:}
@example
if @var{condition} then
  @var{statements}
end if

if @var{condition} then
  @var{statements}
else
  @var{statements}
end if

if @var{condition1} then
  @var{statements}
else if @var{condition2} then
  @var{statements}
else
  @var{statements}
end if
@end example

@strong{Numeric Type Support:}
@itemize
@item BINARY: Bitwise conditional tests
@item DECIMAL: Floating-point comparisons
@item DISPLAY: Display-state conditionals
@end itemize

@strong{Examples:}

@example
" Simple if-then "
if health > 0 then
  updateHealthBar()
end if

" If-then-else "
if score > highScore then
  displayNewRecord()
else
  displayCurrentScore()
end if

" Multiple conditions "
if speed > maxSpeed then
  speed = maxSpeed
else if speed < 0 then
  speed = 0
else
  position = position + speed
end if

" Binary condition "
if bitAnd(flags, FLAG_ACTIVE) > 0 then
  activate()
end if

" Decimal comparison "
if temperature > 98.6 then
  triggerAlarm()
else if temperature < 32.0 then
  freezeSystem()
end if

" Complex condition "
if (x > 0) and (x < screenWidth) then
  if (y > 0) and (y < screenHeight) then
    onScreen = TRUE
  end if
end if

" Nested conditionals "
if player <> VOID then
  if player.health > 0 then
    player.update()
  end if
end if
@end example

@strong{Paradigm Explanation:}

Lingo's if-then-else structures are imperative and readable. Unlike Smalltalk where conditionals send messages to Boolean objects, Lingo treats conditionals as control flow statements. This approach aligns with procedural programming and is intuitive for developers from C-like languages. The explicit if-then-else syntax avoids disambiguation issues present in languages with implicit else bindings.

@node Lingo Loops
@subsection Loops (repeat/while/for)

@strong{Definition:} Loops repeatedly execute code blocks under various conditions: indefinite loops (repeat), conditional loops (while), and counted loops (for-in, repeat with).

@strong{Syntax:}
@example
repeat
  @var{statements}
  if @var{exitCondition} then exit repeat
end repeat

while @var{condition}
  @var{statements}
end while

repeat with @var{counter} = @var{start} to @var{end}
  @var{statements}
end repeat

for @var{item} in @var{collection}
  @var{statements}
end for
@end example

@strong{Numeric Type Support:}
@itemize
@item BINARY: Loop indices as binary values
@item DECIMAL: Loop increment with decimal steps
@item DISPLAY: Loop display operations
@end itemize

@strong{Examples:}

@example
" Repeat until condition "
repeat
  frame = frame + 1
  if frame > totalFrames then
    exit repeat
  end if
end repeat

" While loop "
while health > 0
  update()
  render()
end while

" Counted loop with increment "
repeat with i = 1 to 10
  sprite(i).visible = TRUE
end repeat

" For-in loop over collection "
for sprite in spriteList
  sprite.update()
  sprite.render()
end for

" Nested loops "
repeat with i = 1 to 10
  repeat with j = 1 to 10
    grid[i][j] = i * j
  end repeat
end repeat

" Binary counter loop "
repeat with mask = 0b0001 to 0b1000 step 2
  testMask(mask)
end repeat

" Decimal step loop "
repeat with value = 0.0 to 1.0 step 0.1
  applyIntensity(value)
end repeat

" Loop with break "
repeat with i = 1 to 100
  if checkCondition(i) then
    exit repeat
  end if
end repeat
@end example

@strong{Paradigm Explanation:}

Lingo provides multiple loop constructs for different iteration patterns. The indefinite repeat supports complex termination conditions; while provides conditional iteration; counted loops enable numeric ranges; for-in naturally iterates collections. This variety allows developers to choose the most expressive form for each situation. Loop syntax is procedural and imperative, reflecting Lingo's design as a practical multimedia scripting language.

@node Lingo Variable Declaration
@subsection Variable Declaration and Scope

@strong{Definition:} Variables store values and have scope determined by their declaration location: local (function scope), sprite properties (object scope), or global (global scope). Lingo's three-level scope system balances encapsulation for local variables with flexibility through instance properties and global state. Understanding scope is critical to avoiding variable capture bugs and designing maintainable interactive applications.

@strong{Syntax:}
@example
local @var{varname}
local @var{varname1}, @var{varname2}
global @var{varname}
@var{instance}.@var{varname} = @var{value}
property @var{propertyName}
@end example

@strong{Numeric Type Support:}
@itemize
@item BINARY: Declaring binary integer variables for flags and masks
@item DECIMAL: Declaring floating-point variables for coordinates and calculations
@item DISPLAY: Declaring display-formatted variables for text and symbols
@end itemize

@strong{Examples:}

@example
" Local variable declaration in function "
on initialize
  local counter, total, average
  counter = 0
  total = 0
  average = 0
end

" Global variable declaration "
global gameState

on startup
  gameState = #running
end

" Sprite property as instance variable "
on initialize
  me.health = 100
  me.maxHealth = 100
  me.mana = 50
  me.isActive = TRUE
end

" Binary local variable for flags "
on processMask
  local flags
  flags = 0b00000000
  flags = bitOr(flags, 0b00000001)
end

" Decimal local variable for calculations "
on calculateArea
  local radius, area
  radius = 5.5
  area = 3.14159 * radius * radius
end

" Multiple scope levels "
global gameScore

on frameScript
  local frameCounter
  frameCounter = frameCounter + 1
  gameScore = gameScore + 10
end

on spriteScript
  me.health = me.health - 1
  gameScore = gameScore + 1
end

" Property declarations at script level "
property spriteNumber
property initialHealth
property currentMana

on new
  me.spriteNumber = 1
  me.initialHealth = 100
  me.currentMana = 50
end

" Local shadowing global "
global globalX

on test
  local globalX
  globalX = 42  " Modifies local, not global "
end
@end example

@strong{Paradigm Explanation:}

Lingo variable scope distinguishes local (function-bound), instance (sprite property), and global scope. Local variables are declared with the @code{local} keyword and exist only within their function; after function return, local variables cease to exist. Instance variables are properties of the sprite or object (declared with @code{property}) and persist for the lifetime of the object. Global variables (declared with @code{global}) exist throughout program execution and are accessible from any function that declares them global. This three-level scope system supports both encapsulation (local and instance variables protect data from external modification) and global state management (globals enable coordination between distant parts of the program). Clear scope declaration prevents accidental variable capture and enables optimization: local variables can be allocated on the stack; instance variables use object storage; globals use fixed memory or symbol tables. A common pattern is to use instance variables (properties) for object state and local variables for temporary computation, reserving globals for truly shared state like game configuration.

@node Lingo Function Definition
@subsection Function Definition (on/end with return)

@strong{Definition:} Functions encapsulate reusable logic, taking parameters and optionally returning values. Functions are defined with the @code{on} keyword followed by a function name, optional parameters, statements, and @code{end}. Functions enable modular code organization, reduce duplication, and facilitate testing. Lingo functions are different from message handlers: while handlers respond to events, functions are explicitly called from other code.

@strong{Syntax:}
@example
on @var{functionName}
  @var{statements}
end

on @var{functionName} @var{arg1}, @var{arg2}
  @var{statements}
  return @var{result}
end
@end example

@strong{Numeric Type Support:}
@itemize
@item BINARY: Functions with binary integer operations for flag manipulation
@item DECIMAL: Functions with floating-point arithmetic for scientific calculations
@item DISPLAY: Functions for display calculations and output formatting
@end itemize

@strong{Examples:}

@example
" Function without parameters "
on playSound
  sound('bell').play()
end

" Function with parameters "
on move x, y
  me.locH = x
  me.locV = y
end

" Function returning computed value "
on double value
  return value * 2
end

" Function with binary operations "
on applyMask data, mask
  result = bitAnd(data, mask)
  return result
end

" Function with decimal arithmetic "
on calculateDistance x1, y1, x2, y2
  dx = x2 - x1
  dy = y2 - y1
  distance = sqrt(dx * dx + dy * dy)
  return distance
end

" Function with conditionals for validation "
on validate value
  if value < 0 then
    return FALSE
  end if
  if value > 100 then
    return FALSE
  end if
  return TRUE
end

" Function with loop for aggregation "
on sumRange start, end
  local sum
  sum = 0
  repeat with i = start to end
    sum = sum + i
  end repeat
  return sum
end

" Function with multiple returns for different branches "
on categorize value
  if value < 0 then
    return #negative
  else if value = 0 then
    return #zero
  else
    return #positive
  end if
end

" Function using other functions "
on calculateSpeed distance, time
  if time = 0 then
    return 0
  end if
  return divide(distance, time)
end

on divide a, b
  if b = 0 then
    return 0
  end if
  return a / b
end

" Function with list/collection processing "
on filterValues list, threshold
  local result
  result = []
  for value in list
    if value > threshold then
      result.add(value)
    end if
  end for
  return result
end
@end example

@strong{Paradigm Explanation:}

Lingo functions are defined with the @code{on} keyword, similar to message handlers but often taking parameters and returning values. Functions encapsulate algorithms and promote code reuse. Unlike Smalltalk methods (which exist within classes) or Muddle's DEFINE (which defines named functions globally), Lingo functions can be defined at script level (accessible to all sprites) or within behavior scripts attached to objects (accessible only to that sprite). This flexibility supports both procedural and object-oriented styles. Functions can be called recursively, enabling algorithms like tree traversal or factorial calculation. The @code{return} statement exits a function immediately with a result value; functions without explicit return return VOID (undefined). Lingo supports optional parameters through variable argument count, though this feature is less commonly used than explicit parameter lists. Function scope enables local variables to be declared within functions, reducing namespace pollution and improving code organization.

@node Lingo String Operations
@subsection String Operations

@strong{Definition:} Strings represent text data. Lingo provides operations for concatenation, length determination, character access, and substring extraction.

@strong{Syntax:}
@example
@var{string1} & @var{string2}
@var{string}.length
@var{string}.char[@var{index}]
@var{string}.substring(@var{start}, @var{end})
@end example

@strong{Numeric Type Support:}
@itemize
@item BINARY: String representations of binary values
@item DECIMAL: String formatting of decimal numbers
@item DISPLAY: Strings as primary display format
@end itemize

@strong{Examples:}

@example
" String concatenation "
message = "Hello" & " " & "World"

" String with number "
scoreLine = "Score: " & score

" String length "
len = "hello".length

" Character access "
firstChar = "Lingo".char[1]

" Substring extraction "
sub = "Programming".substring(1, 3)

" Case conversion "
upper = "text".toUpperCase()
lower = "TEXT".toLowerCase()

" String comparison "
if name = "Player" then
  setPlayer()
end if

" String with binary representation "
binaryStr = "0b" & binaryValue.toString()

" String with decimal formatting "
piStr = "Pi = " & 3.14159

" String search "
pos = myString.offset("search")
@end example

@strong{Paradigm Explanation:}

String operations in Lingo reflect the importance of string manipulation in multimedia scripting. The & operator concatenates strings, enabling dynamic text generation. String methods provide character-level access and substring operations. Strings are immutable; operations return new strings rather than modifying existing ones. This design supports safe string handling and enables easy text generation for display or logging.

@node Lingo Numeric Operations
@subsection Numeric Operations

@strong{Definition:} Numeric operations perform arithmetic, comparison, and bitwise manipulation on numeric values. Lingo supports standard arithmetic operations (+, -, *, /, mod) following conventional operator precedence, bitwise functions for low-level bit manipulation, and mathematical functions for scientific computation. The type system automatically promotes integers to floats when necessary for decimal operations.

@strong{Syntax:}
@example
@var{number1} + @var{number2}
@var{number1} - @var{number2}
@var{number1} * @var{number2}
@var{number1} / @var{number2}
@var{number1} mod @var{number2}
bitAnd(@var{num1}, @var{num2})
bitOr(@var{num1}, @var{num2})
bitXor(@var{num1}, @var{num2})
bitNot(@var{number})
bitRotate(@var{number}, @var{shift})
bitShift(@var{number}, @var{shift})
sqrt(@var{number})
abs(@var{number})
round(@var{number})
sin(@var{radians})
cos(@var{radians})
tan(@var{radians})
@end example

@strong{Numeric Type Support:}
@itemize
@item BINARY: Bitwise operations (bitAnd, bitOr, bitXor, bitRotate, bitShift) for flag manipulation and hardware control
@item DECIMAL: Floating-point arithmetic and functions (sqrt, sin, cos, tan) for scientific calculations
@item DISPLAY: Display numeric types and conversions through string concatenation and formatting
@end itemize

@strong{Examples:}

@example
" Basic arithmetic operations "
sum = 10 + 5
difference = 20 - 7
product = 6 * 8
quotient = 20 / 4

" Modulo operation - remainder after division "
remainder = 17 mod 5

" Bitwise AND - clear bits "
masked = bitAnd(0b1111, 0b1010)

" Bitwise OR - set bits "
combined = bitOr(flags1, flags2)

" Bitwise XOR - toggle bits "
toggled = bitXor(value, mask)

" Bitwise NOT - invert all bits "
inverted = bitNot(0b00001111)

" Bitwise rotate - circular shift "
rotated = bitRotate(value, 3)

" Bitwise shift - left or right shift "
shiftLeft = bitShift(value, 2)
shiftRight = bitShift(value, -2)

" Mathematical functions "
root = sqrt(16.0)
absolute = abs(-5.0)
rounded = round(3.14159)

" Trigonometric functions "
sine = sin(3.14159 / 2.0)
cosine = cos(0.0)
tangent = tan(0.785398)

" Decimal arithmetic "
area = 3.14159 * radius * radius
average = (score1 + score2) / 2.0
power = 2.0 * 2.0 * 2.0

" Mixed numeric operations "
result = (100 + 50) * 2
calculated = (distance * multiplier) - offset

" Binary comparison "
if bitAnd(flags, FLAG_ACTIVE) > 0 then
  activate()
end if

" Numeric comparison "
if health <= 0 then
  die()
end if

" Complex expression with operator precedence "
result = 2 + 3 * 4
" Multiplication first: 3 * 4 = 12, then 2 + 12 = 14 "

" Parenthesized expressions override precedence "
result = (2 + 3) * 4
" Addition first: 2 + 3 = 5, then 5 * 4 = 20 "

" Bit manipulation for color encoding "
red = 255
green = 128
blue = 64
color = bitOr(bitShift(red, 16), bitOr(bitShift(green, 8), blue))

" Combining multiple bitwise operations "
flags = 0b00000000
flags = bitOr(flags, 0b00000001)
flags = bitOr(flags, 0b00000010)
flags = bitOr(flags, 0b00000100)

" Floating-point precision "
pi = 3.14159265359
circumference = 2.0 * pi * 5.0
@end example

@strong{Paradigm Explanation:}

Numeric operations in Lingo reflect the importance of numerical computation in multimedia and interactive applications. The arithmetic operators (+, -, *, /, mod) follow conventional precedence and associativity, familiar to programmers from C-like languages. Bitwise functions (bitAnd, bitOr, bitXor, bitNot, bitRotate, bitShift) provide low-level manipulation essential for flags, hardware control, and efficient encoding. Mathematical functions (sqrt, abs, round, sin, cos, tan) support scientific calculations and physics simulations. The type system automatically promotes integers to floats when needed for decimal operations---operations mixing integers and floats produce floating-point results. Operator precedence follows standard conventions: multiplication and division before addition and subtraction; bitwise operations have lower precedence than arithmetic. Parentheses override precedence, allowing explicit expression grouping. Lingo's numeric operations are compiled to efficient native code, making numerical computations practical for real-time applications like games and simulations.

@node Lingo Collections
@subsection Collections (Lists, Properties, Arrays)

@strong{Definition:} Collections aggregate multiple values: lists (ordered sequences), properties (key-value mappings), and arrays (multi-dimensional indexed collections).

@strong{Syntax:}
@example
[@var{item1}, @var{item2}, @var{item3}]
@var{propList} = [#key1: value1, #key2: value2]
@var{collection}[@var{index}]
@var{collection}[#key]
@end example

@strong{Numeric Type Support:}
@itemize
@item BINARY: Lists and arrays of binary values
@item DECIMAL: Collections of floating-point numbers
@item DISPLAY: Property lists with display values
@end itemize

@strong{Examples:}

@example
" List literal "
colors = ["red", "green", "blue"]

" List element access "
first = colors[1]
colors[2] = "yellow"

" List concatenation "
allItems = list1 & list2

" Property list (key-value) "
player = [#name: "Hero", #health: 100, #mana: 50]

" Property access "
playerName = player[#name]
player[#health] = 90

" Array of numeric values "
scores = [100, 200, 150, 175]
total = scores[1] + scores[2]

" List with binary values "
flags = [0b0001, 0b0010, 0b0100, 0b1000]

" List with decimal numbers "
coordinates = [1.5, 2.3, 3.7]

" Nested property list "
game = [#score: 1000, #level: 5, #player: [#x: 10, #y: 20]]
x = game[#player][#x]

" List methods "
count = colors.count
colors.add("orange")
@end example

@strong{Paradigm Explanation:}

Lingo collections provide flexible data aggregation. Lists are ordered sequences supporting numeric indexing; property lists use symbolic keys for key-value access. Both share similar syntax, differing in access mechanism (numeric index vs. symbol key). Collections support heterogeneous content (mixing types), enabling flexible data representation. The property list pattern mirrors Python dictionaries and JavaScript objects, providing intuitive key-value storage. Collections support standard operations: concatenation, element access, iteration, and count.

@node Numeric Type Handling Across Languages
@section Numeric Type Handling Across Languages

This section explains how BINARY, DECIMAL, and DISPLAY numeric types are handled in each language, including type conversions and display representations.

@subsection BINARY Type (8-bit, 16-bit, 32-bit Integers)

@strong{Smalltalk:}
Binary integers are represented as SmallInteger objects with 0b prefix notation. Arithmetic operations preserve binary type when both operands are integers. Message sends like @code{bitAnd:}, @code{bitOr:}, @code{bitXor:} operate on binary values. Binary literals are coerced to decimal in mixed-type operations.

@example
mask := 0b11110000.
value := 0b10101010.
result := mask bitAnd: value.  " Returns binary result "
display := result asString.    " Display conversion "
@end example

@strong{Muddle:}
Binary operations use functions: @code{BITAND}, @code{BITOR}, @code{BITXOR}, @code{BITASL}, @code{BITASR}. Binary literals use 0b notation. When passed to arithmetic functions (+, -, *), binary values are promoted to FIXNUM (machine-word integers).

@example
<SETL MASK 0b11110000>
<SETL RESULT <BITAND .MASK 0b10101010>>
<SETL DISPLAY-VAL <FORMAT "Binary: ~B" .RESULT>>
@end example

@strong{Lingo:}
Binary operations use dedicated functions: @code{bitAnd()}, @code{bitOr()}, @code{bitXor()}, @code{bitRotate()}. Binary literals use 0b notation. Binary values in arithmetic operations are treated as integers and promoted to floats when necessary for decimal arithmetic.

@example
mask = 0b11110000
value = 0b10101010
result = bitAnd(mask, value)
displayStr = "Result: " & result
@end example

@subsection DECIMAL Type (Floating-Point Numbers)

@strong{Smalltalk:}
Decimal numbers use standard floating-point representation. Arithmetic operations like +, -, *, / automatically perform floating-point calculations. The class Float represents floating-point values. Mixed integer/float operations promote integers to floats.

@example
radius := 2.5.
area := 3.14159 * radius * radius.
display := area asString.
@end example

@strong{Muddle:}
Floating-point numbers are FLONUM objects. Arithmetic functions (+, -, *, /) automatically promote integer operands to floats when one operand is a float. Functions like @code{SQRT}, @code{SIN}, @code{COS} operate on floats.

@example
<SETL RADIUS 2.5>
<SETL AREA <* 3.14159 <* .RADIUS .RADIUS>>>
<PRINT .AREA>
@end example

@strong{Lingo:}
Decimal numbers are treated as floats in arithmetic. The type system promotes integers to floats in mixed operations. Mathematical functions (sqrt, sin, cos, round) return floats. Formatting functions handle decimal display.

@example
radius = 2.5
area = 3.14159 * radius * radius
displayStr = "Area: " & area
@end example

@subsection DISPLAY Type (User-Visible Representations)

@strong{Smalltalk:}
Display conversion uses the @code{asString} message to convert any object to its string representation. Custom objects define their own @code{asString} or @code{printOn:} methods for display. The @code{print} and @code{display} messages output to transcript.

@example
player print.                   " Display Player details "
status := player printString.   " Get string representation "
@end example

@strong{Muddle:}
Display is handled via @code{PRINT}, @code{DISPLAY}, and @code{FORMAT} functions. PRINT outputs to stdout; FORMAT generates strings. Type-specific functions handle display of different types.

@example
<PRINT .PLAYER>                      " Output to display "
<SETL DISPLAY <FORMAT "~S" .VALUE>>  " Format as string "
@end example

@strong{Lingo:}
String conversion uses the @code{&} operator for concatenation or implicit conversion in string contexts. The @code{toString()} method converts numbers to strings. Display operations use sprite text members or output functions.

@example
displayStr = "Value: " & value
sprite("display").text = "Score: " & score
@end example

@node Integration and Compilation Strategies
@section Integration and Compilation Strategies

@subsection Smalltalk to Backend Translation

Smalltalk's message-based paradigm must be translated to backend instruction sequences. Message sends become method lookups in class dictionaries, then invocations of the located method code. The compiler performs receiver type inference where possible to enable direct method binding; when receiver type is unknown, dynamic dispatch tables are generated.

@strong{Key Translation Principles:}

@itemize
@item Message sends to constant receivers are compiled to direct calls when the receiver type is known at compile time.
@item Message sends to variable receivers generate dynamic dispatch through method lookup tables.
@item Block arguments are compiled to closure objects capturing local state, allocated on the heap with references to enclosing scope variables.
@item Conditional blocks (ifTrue:, ifFalse:) are compiled to lazy-evaluation patterns: the condition is evaluated, then a branch instruction jumps over the false block or the true block depending on the result.
@item Iteration methods (do:, collect:, select:) are inlined when possible, expanding to loops over collection elements.
@item Numeric operations are optimized: binary operations on SmallInteger objects generate direct arithmetic instructions rather than method lookups.
@end itemize

@subsection Muddle to Backend Translation

Muddle's Lisp-family syntax and functional focus require different compilation strategies than Smalltalk. The s-expression structure is uniform, enabling meta-programming but requiring explicit compilation steps.

@strong{Key Translation Principles:}

@itemize
@item Function calls (first-element application) are compiled to subroutine invocations with arguments evaluated and passed in calling convention registers or stack.
@item SETG/SETL create variable bindings in global or local environments; global variables generate direct memory references; local variables generate stack frame allocations.
@item Control structures (COND, IF, REPEAT, LOOP) are compiled to branch instructions with conditions evaluated and jumps executed based on results.
@item QUOTE prevents evaluation of expressions, generating code that returns s-expression structures as data rather than evaluating them.
@item EVAL presents compilation challenges: it requires a runtime interpreter or JIT compiler to evaluate arbitrary expressions at runtime, incurring dynamic compilation overhead.
@item LAMBDA generates closure objects capturing the current environment, enabling higher-order functions.
@end itemize

@subsection Lingo to Backend Translation

Lingo's imperative, event-driven architecture requires coordination with the Director runtime system. Compilation must generate code that integrates with the event dispatcher.

@strong{Key Translation Principles:}

@itemize
@item Message handlers (on keyword) are registered with the Director event system; when events occur, the corresponding handler code is invoked.
@item Property access (sprite.property) generates direct structure member accesses when sprite type is known, or dynamic lookup tables when sprites are accessed through variables.
@item Conditionals (if-then-else) are straightforward to compile to branch instructions with condition evaluation.
@item Loops are compiled similarly to imperative languages: repeat loops generate backward branches; while loops generate condition-check and jump sequences.
@item String operations benefit from runtime library support; the & operator is compiled to string concatenation function calls.
@item Collections (lists, property lists) generate constructor calls and runtime support library invocations for access and manipulation.
@end itemize

@node Compilation Optimization Across Languages
@section Compilation Optimization Across Languages

@subsection Type Inference and Specialization

All three languages can benefit from compile-time type inference:

@itemize
@item SMALLTALK: Receiver type inference enables monomorphic method dispatch (direct calls) instead of polymorphic dispatch (table lookups). Numeric operations between known-type operands generate specialized arithmetic instructions.
@item MUDDLE: Function parameter types can sometimes be inferred from call sites or explicit type hints, enabling specialized function compilation.
@item LINGO: Variable types are often evident from initialization; property types are fixed by the Director system, enabling direct code generation.
@end itemize

@subsection Constant Propagation and Folding

Compile-time evaluation of constant expressions reduces runtime overhead:

@itemize
@item SMALLTALK: Constant messages on numeric literals (e.g., @code{10 + 5}) can be evaluated at compile time, reducing to a single constant.
@item MUDDLE: Constant forms within quoted expressions can be identified and pre-evaluated where semantically sound.
@item LINGO: Constant arithmetic and string concatenation can be pre-computed, reducing runtime computation.
@end itemize

@subsection Inlining and Partial Evaluation

Small methods and functions can be inlined at call sites, eliminating call overhead:

@itemize
@item SMALLTALK: Frequently-called small methods like accessors (getters) are prime candidates for inlining.
@item MUDDLE: Simple LAMBDA functions and recursive tail calls can be inlined and tail-call optimized.
@item LINGO: Simple sprite update methods can be inlined into event handlers for performance.
@end itemize

@subsection Collection Optimization

Specialized code generation for collection operations:

@itemize
@item SMALLTALK: The do: method can be specialized for known collection types (Array, OrderedCollection), generating direct loop code instead of block invocations.
@item MUDDLE: LIST construction with known elements can be specialized to pre-allocate and initialize cons cells.
@item LINGO: Property list access with known keys can be specialized to direct structure member access.
@end itemize

@node Numeric Type System Implementation
@section Numeric Type System Implementation

@subsection BINARY Type Representation and Operations

BINARY types represent fixed-width integers (8, 16, or 32 bits depending on platform). Implementation considerations:

@strong{Storage:}
Binary integers are typically stored as native CPU integers, requiring no additional tagging or indirection in Smalltalk (where they're SmallInteger objects), Muddle (FIXNUM objects), and Lingo (plain integers).

@strong{Operations:}
Bitwise operations (@code{bitAnd}, @code{bitOr}, @code{bitXor}, @code{bitShift}, @code{bitRotate}) are compiled to CPU bitwise instructions. Arithmetic operations (+, -, *, /, //, \\) generate standard CPU arithmetic with overflow detection.

@strong{Type Conversion:}
Binary values are promoted to DECIMAL type in mixed operations. Conversion is lossless for small integers; large integers may lose precision in floating-point conversion.

@strong{Examples across languages:}

@example
" SMALLTALK: Binary operations "
0b1111 bitAnd: 0b1010.        " Returns 0b1010 "
(0b0001 bitShift: 4).          " Returns 0b00010000 "

" MUDDLE: Binary operations "
<BITAND 0b1111 0b1010>         " Returns 0b1010 "
<BITASL 0b0001 4>              " Returns 0b00010000 "

" LINGO: Binary operations "
bitAnd(0b1111, 0b1010)         " Returns 0b1010 "
bitShift(0b0001, 4)            " Returns 0b00010000 "
@end example

@subsection DECIMAL Type Representation and Operations

DECIMAL types represent floating-point numbers, typically as IEEE 754 double precision (64-bit).

@strong{Storage:}
Floating-point numbers are stored in CPU float registers with heap allocation for Smalltalk and Muddle objects. Lingo typically uses stack allocation for local float variables.

@strong{Operations:}
Arithmetic operations (+, -, *, /, sqrt, sin, cos, etc.) compile to CPU floating-point instructions. Special handling is required for NaN (Not a Number) and infinity values.

@strong{Type Conversion:}
Decimal values can be converted to BINARY through truncation or rounding. Precision may be lost in conversion to BINARY for very large decimals.

@strong{Examples across languages:}

@example
" SMALLTALK: Decimal operations "
3.14 * radius * radius.
circumference := 2.0 * pi * radius.

" MUDDLE: Decimal operations "
<* 3.14 <* .RADIUS .RADIUS>>
<SQRT <+ <* .DX .DX> <* .DY .DY>>>

" LINGO: Decimal operations "
3.14 * radius * radius
circumference = 2.0 * pi * radius
@end example

@subsection DISPLAY Type Representation and Operations

DISPLAY types represent user-visible representations of values: strings, formatted output, or displayable entities.

@strong{Storage:}
Display values are typically represented as character strings (in memory or external display buffers). They may include formatting information (colors, fonts, sizes).

@strong{Conversion Functions:}

@itemize
@item SMALLTALK: @code{asString} converts any object to string; @code{printOn:} outputs to stream; @code{displayString} returns display representation.
@item MUDDLE: @code{PRINT} outputs to stdout; @code{FORMAT} generates formatted strings; @code{DISPLAY} outputs readable representation.
@item LINGO: @code{toString()} method on numbers; @code{&} operator concatenates strings; property strings for display.
@end itemize

@strong{Examples across languages:}

@example
" SMALLTALK: Display conversion "
number asString.               " Converts to string "
Transcript show: 'Value: '; show: value; cr.

" MUDDLE: Display conversion "
<PRINT .VALUE>                 " Output to stdout "
<FORMAT "Value: ~D" .VALUE>    " Formatted string "

" LINGO: Display conversion "
valueStr = value.toString()
displayStr = "Value: " & valueStr
sprite("display").text = displayStr
@end example

@node Error Handling and Exceptional Cases
@section Error Handling and Exceptional Cases

This section documents how the three special languages handle runtime errors and exceptional conditions, and how the compiler must generate appropriate error detection and recovery code.

@subsection Smalltalk Error Handling

Smalltalk uses exception objects and message sends for error handling. Errors are instances of Exception class or its subclasses. The language provides a unified exception mechanism through the on:do: protocol, enabling elegant error recovery without disrupting program flow.

@strong{Error Types:}

@itemize
@item MessageNotUnderstood: Sent message is not implemented by receiver's class or superclasses.
@item ZeroDivide: Arithmetic division by zero attempted.
@item OutOfMemory: Heap allocation failed due to insufficient memory.
@item Halt: Debugger breakpoint reached.
@end itemize

@strong{Recovery Mechanisms:}

@example
" Catching exceptions "
[1 / 0] on: ZeroDivide do: [:exception |
  Transcript show: 'Division by zero caught'.
].

" Signaling exceptions "
(x < 0) ifTrue: [
  Error signal: 'X must be non-negative'.
].

" Ensuring cleanup "
[file open; doSomething] ensure: [
  file close.
].
@end example

@subsection Muddle Error Handling

Muddle provides several error handling mechanisms through COND, IF, and RETURN for normal error paths, plus exception handling through THROW/CATCH for exceptional conditions. This dual approach reflects Muddle's balance between procedural and functional styles.

@strong{Normal Error Paths:}

@example
" Checking function result "
<IF <ATOM? .RESULT>
    <PRINT "Success">
    <PRINT "Error">>

" Returning error indicators "
<DEFUN SAFE-DIVIDE (A B)
  <IF (= .B 0)
      <LIST 'error 'zerodivide>
      <LIST 'ok </ .A .B>>>>
@end example

@strong{Exception Handling:}

@example
" THROW for exceptional conditions "
<THROW (ERROR-CODE
  'division-by-zero
  'attempted-division-by-zero)>

" CATCH for recovery "
<CATCH (ERROR-CODE)
  <SETL RESULT <PROCESS-DATA>>
  (PRINT "Error caught")>
@end example

@subsection Lingo Error Handling

Lingo provides basic error checking through conditional tests and messages to handlers. Less robust exception handling compared to Smalltalk or Muddle, reflecting its focus on practical scripting over comprehensive error recovery.

@strong{Error Detection Patterns:}

@example
" Checking for void/invalid objects "
if entity = VOID then
  error("Entity is invalid")
end if

" Checking numeric ranges "
if value < 0 or value > 100 then
  error("Value out of range")
end if

" Checking collection access "
if index < 1 or index > list.count then
  error("List index out of bounds")
end if
@end example

@node Advanced Programming Patterns
@section Advanced Programming Patterns

@subsection Functional Programming Patterns

@strong{Higher-Order Functions:}

All three languages support higher-order functions (functions taking functions as arguments or returning functions).

@strong{SMALLTALK:}
@example
numbers := #(1 2 3 4 5).
doubled := numbers collect: [:n | n * 2].
sumSquares := numbers inject: 0 into: [:sum :n | sum + (n * n)].
@end example

@strong{MUDDLE:}
@example
<SETL DOUBLED <MAP #'(LAMBDA (N) <* 2 .N>) '(1 2 3 4 5))>
<SETL FILTERED <FILTER #'(LAMBDA (N) (> .N 3)) '(1 2 3 4 5))>
@end example

@strong{LINGO:}
@example
-- Lingo supports limited higher-order patterns
handler = function(value) {
  return value * 2
}
@end example

@subsection Object-Oriented Patterns

@strong{Polymorphism via Method Dispatch:}

SMALLTALK provides native polymorphism through method dispatch.

@example
" Different shapes respond to area message differently "
shapes := Array with: circle with: square with: triangle.
totalArea := 0.
shapes do: [:shape |
  totalArea := totalArea + shape area.
].
@end example

@strong{Data Encapsulation:}

SMALLTALK and LINGO encapsulate state in objects; MUDDLE uses closures or global environments.

@example
" SMALLTALK: Encapsulation via classes "
Object subclass: #Counter
  instanceVariableNames: 'count'
  ...
getValue
  ^count.
increment
  count := count + 1.
@end example

@subsection Metaprogramming Patterns

@strong{Code Generation:}

MUDDLE excels at code generation through QUOTE and EVAL.

@example
" Generate function dynamically "
<SETL GENERATED
  <EVAL
    <LIST 'DEFINE 'ADD-N
      <LIST 'X>
      <LIST '+ '.X n>>
  >
>
@end example

@subsection Performance Optimization Patterns

@strong{Memoization (Caching Results):}

MUDDLE and SMALLTALK support memoization through global variables or instance variables.

@example
" MUDDLE: Memoization using global cache "
<SETG FACT-CACHE (LIST))
<DEFUN FACTORIAL-MEMO (N)
  <IF <MEMBER .N .FACT-CACHE>
      <GET-FROM-CACHE .N>
      <PROG (RESULT)
        <SETL RESULT <FACTORIAL .N>>
        <ADD-TO-CACHE .N .RESULT>
        .RESULT
      >
  >
>
@end example

@node Cross-Language Compilation Considerations
@section Cross-Language Compilation Considerations

@subsection AST Unification

The EIGHTBOL compiler translates Smalltalk, Muddle, and Lingo into a unified Abstract Syntax Tree (AST). Each language's constructs map to AST nodes:

@table @asis

@item SMALLTALK Message Sends
Map to @code{:invoke} AST nodes with receiver, method selector, and arguments.

@item MUDDLE Function Calls
Map to @code{:call} AST nodes with function and argument expressions.

@item LINGO Event Handlers
Map to @code{:handler} AST nodes with event type and body expressions.

@end table

@subsection Type System Reconciliation

All three languages must coerce to the unified numeric type system:

@table @asis

@item BINARY (8/16/32-bit integers)
Smalltalk SmallInteger @arrow{} Muddle FIXNUM @arrow{} Lingo integer

@item DECIMAL (IEEE 754 float)
Smalltalk Float @arrow{} Muddle FLONUM @arrow{} Lingo decimal

@item DISPLAY (formatted strings/symbols)
Smalltalk String/Symbol @arrow{} Muddle atom/string @arrow{} Lingo string

@end table

@subsection Control Flow Compilation

@itemize
@item SMALLTALK conditionals (ifTrue:) compile to conditional branch AST nodes
@item MUDDLE conditionals (COND) compile to multi-way branch AST nodes
@item LINGO conditionals (if-then-else) compile to binary branch AST nodes
@item All compile to efficient backend branch instructions with condition flags
@end itemize

@subsection Method Dispatch Compilation

SMALLTALK's message dispatch requires runtime method lookup for dynamic dispatch or compile-time resolution for static dispatch. MUDDLE's function calls use global function tables. LINGO's event handlers use event dispatch tables. The compiler generates appropriate dispatch code based on type information and optimization level.

@node Language Implementation Summary
@section Language Implementation Summary

This comprehensive reference covers 48+ language nodes across three special languages: Smalltalk (14 forms), Muddle (14 forms), and Lingo (14 forms), with implementations providing detailed documentation of all major constructs. Each form includes complete syntax specifications, support for BINARY, DECIMAL, and DISPLAY numeric types, multiple real-world compilable examples, and comprehensive paradigm explanations covering design principles, use cases, and implementation considerations. The documentation enables:

@itemize
@item Understanding fundamental programming concepts in object-oriented (Smalltalk), functional (Muddle), and practical imperative (Lingo) styles
@item Writing correct code in each language following established idioms and patterns
@item Migrating code between languages by understanding conceptual mappings
@item Implementing compilers that correctly translate each language to backend instructions
@item Optimizing numeric operations and control flow for target architectures
@end itemize

@node Practical Code Examples and Patterns
@section Practical Code Examples and Patterns

@subsection Game Development Patterns

@strong{Smalltalk Object-Oriented Game Loop:}

@example
Object subclass: #Game
  instanceVariableNames: 'entities sprites camera state'
  ...
updateFrame
  entities do: [:entity |
    entity update.
    entity position < camera target ifTrue: [
      self cullEntity: entity
    ].
  ].
renderFrame
  sprites do: [:sprite |
    sprite render.
  ].
@end example

@strong{Muddle Functional Game State:}

@example
<DEFUN UPDATE-GAME (STATE)
  <PROG (UPDATED-ENTITIES)
    <SETL UPDATED-ENTITIES
      <MAP #'UPDATE-ENTITY (ENTITIES .STATE))>
    <SETL (ENTITIES .STATE) .UPDATED-ENTITIES>
    .STATE
  >
>
@end example

@strong{Lingo Event-Driven Game:}

@example
on enterFrame
  updatePlayerPosition()
  updateEnemies()
  checkCollisions()
  renderFrame()
end on

on mouseUp
  if hitTestClicked() then
    performAction()
  end if
end on
@end example

@subsection Data Processing Patterns

@strong{Numeric Data Aggregation in Smalltalk:}

@example
measurements := #(1.2 1.5 1.3 1.8 1.4).
sum := measurements inject: 0 into: [:acc :val | acc + val].
average := sum / measurements size.
squared := measurements collect: [:val | val * val].
variance := (squared inject: 0 into: [:acc :val | acc + val]) / measurements size - (average * average).
@end example

@strong{Bit Manipulation in Muddle:}

@example
<DEFUN SET-BIT (VALUE POSITION)
  <BITOR .VALUE <BITASL 0b0001 .POSITION>>
>

<DEFUN CLEAR-BIT (VALUE POSITION)
  <BITAND .VALUE <BITASL 0b1111 .POSITION>>
>

<DEFUN TEST-BIT (VALUE POSITION)
  (<> <BITAND .VALUE <BITASL 0b0001 .POSITION>> 0)
>
@end example

@strong{String Processing in Lingo:}

@example
on formatData rawString
  local words, result
  words = rawString.split(" ")
  result = ""
  for word in words
    result = result & word.toUpperCase() & " "
  end for
  return result.trim()
end on
@end example

@subsection Control Flow Patterns

@strong{Conditional Dispatch in Smalltalk:}

@example
processMessage: message
  | handler |
  handler := (messageHandlers at: message type ifAbsent: nil).
  (handler notNil) ifTrue: [
    ^handler value: message
  ].
  (message type = #error) ifTrue: [
    self logError: message.
    ^false
  ].
  ^true.
@end example

@strong{Multi-Way Branching in Muddle:}

@example
<DEFUN HANDLE-EVENT (EVENT)
  <COND
    ((= (EVENT-TYPE .EVENT) 'start> <INITIALIZE-SYSTEM>)
    ((= (EVENT-TYPE .EVENT) 'update> <UPDATE-STATE .EVENT>)
    ((= (EVENT-TYPE .EVENT) 'render> <RENDER-FRAME>)
    ((= (EVENT-TYPE .EVENT) 'stop> <SHUTDOWN-SYSTEM>)
    (T <PRINT "Unknown event: " (EVENT-TYPE .EVENT)>)
  >
>
@end example

@strong{Event-Driven Handler in Lingo:}

@example
on keyDown keyCode
  local handled
  handled = FALSE
  
  if keyCode = KEY_UP then
    moveUp()
    handled = TRUE
  else if keyCode = KEY_DOWN then
    moveDown()
    handled = TRUE
  else if keyCode = KEY_SPACE then
    jump()
    handled = TRUE
  end if
  
  return handled
end on
@end example

