@node Language-Specific Optimization Techniques
@section Language-Specific Optimization Techniques

Different languages and their compilation models enable different optimization opportunities. Understanding these language-specific characteristics enables the compiler to generate efficient code tailored to each language's semantics and strengths.

@subsection Smalltalk-Specific Optimizations

@strong{Message Send Optimization:} The compiler can optimize message sends by analyzing receiver types. When a receiver's class is known at compile-time, the compiler can emit direct method calls instead of dynamic dispatch through method lookup tables. For example, arithmetic messages to SmallInteger objects can be compiled to native CPU arithmetic instructions with direct calls to methods, eliminating method lookup overhead.

@strong{Block Inlining:} Iteration methods like @code{do:} with small blocks can be inlined by the compiler. The block code is inserted directly into the loop, eliminating function call overhead. This transformation preserves semantics while improving performance.

@strong{Immutability Analysis:} The compiler can track which objects are never modified, enabling optimizations like constant propagation and dead-code elimination. Immutable collections enable safe sharing and caching.

@subsection Muddle-Specific Optimizations

@strong{Tail-Call Optimization:} Recursive functions can be compiled to iterative code through tail-call optimization (TCO). When a function's last operation is a call to itself, the compiler emits code that reuses the current stack frame for the recursive call, preventing stack overflow and improving performance. This is essential for idiomatic Muddle code relying on recursion.

@strong{Symbolic Constant Folding:} QUOTE prevents evaluation; the compiler can analyze quoted expressions to identify constant values. These compile to inline constants, avoiding runtime evaluation overhead.

@strong{EVAL Specialization:} When EVAL receives a compile-time-constant argument, the compiler can perform evaluation at compile-time, embedding results directly into compiled code. This trades compile-time analysis for runtime performance.

@subsection Lingo-Specific Optimizations

@strong{Property Access Specialization:} Sprite properties accessed with known sprite objects can be compiled to direct structure member access instead of property table lookups. The compiler tracks sprite type information and generates specialized code.

@strong{Event Handler Optimization:} Event handlers can be compiled to be directly invoked by the event dispatcher without going through generic message dispatch. This eliminates indirection for frequently-called handlers.

@strong{String Operation Optimization:} String concatenation with the & operator can be optimized using string builders instead of creating intermediate strings. The compiler detects chains of concatenations and generates code that accumulates results in a single buffer.

