@subsection Implementation Strategies Across Architectures

The EIGHTBOL compiler supports multiple target architectures (6502, Z80, x86, ARM, etc.). Each language compiles to a target-independent intermediate form that backends can optimize. Key considerations:

@itemize
@item Smalltalk message dispatch compiles to method table lookups optimized by target architecture (e.g., using indexed jumps on architectures supporting them)
@item Muddle function calls compile to subroutine calls with arguments passed according to each architecture's calling convention
@item Lingo event handlers compile to direct invocation sequences optimized for the event dispatcher on each target
@item Numeric operations compile to architecture-specific instructions: native 8/16/32-bit arithmetic on 6502, extended precision arithmetic on architectures supporting it
@item Binary operations use bitwise instructions native to the target CPU
@end itemize

The compiler's modular design enables adding new target architectures by implementing architecture-specific code generation without changing the high-level language implementations.

